;; manifest/orgs-detectors.edn — the registry of detectors that CANNOT be fleet
;; gates, and therefore run on this machine instead.
;;
;; ## Why this file exists
;;
;; ADR-2608124800 measured that roughly twenty verification scripts in this
;; workspace cannot be murakumo fleet gates, for a structural reason CLAUDE.md
;; already records: **a fleet gate ships only the target repo's own tree**, so a
;; detector that reads `orgs/` finds an empty workspace on a node and can never
;; go green there. `root-permit-index` has been 0 pass / 282 fail for exactly
;; this reason. Two of the detectors say so in their own headers.
;;
;; So the answer for them is not a `gates.edn` line. It is a home on a machine
;; that actually has `orgs/`. `scripts/orgs-detector-tick.cljs` is that home and
;; this file is what it runs.
;;
;; ## What an entry must declare, and why each field is mandatory
;;
;;   :id            keyword, stable. Findings are keyed under it forever.
;;   :title         one line, shown to a human at session start.
;;   :why           what it would have caught, with a date. An entry that cannot
;;                  answer this is a detector nobody has justified running.
;;   :argv          the command. `{{repo}}` is substituted with the checkout
;;                  holding the tick script, `{{root}}` with the orgs-bearing
;;                  checkout. They differ when the tick runs from a worktree.
;;   :cwd           :root (the orgs-bearing checkout) or :repo.
;;   :findings      :protocol -- the detector prints FINDING<TAB>sev<TAB>key<TAB>detail
;;                  lines. This is the only supported mode, deliberately: a tick
;;                  that regex-scraped prose reports would mis-key findings the
;;                  first time a message was reworded, and silently report every
;;                  standing finding as new.
;;   :evidence      regex that MUST appear in the output for the run to be
;;                  recorded :ok. This is the anti-theatre floor. ADR-2608124800
;;                  found five scripts that print PASSED without measuring; a
;;                  detector that dies after two lines must be recorded
;;                  :inconclusive, never clean. `SCANNED\t0\t...` fails it too.
;;   :interval-ms   minimum age before re-running. Per detector, because the
;;                  measured runtimes differ by 5x.
;;   :timeout-ms    hard kill. No entry may omit it.
;;   :writes        MUST be :none. The tick refuses to run an entry that does
;;                  not declare it. See the invariants below.
;;   :measured      what was actually observed when the entry was added, so the
;;                  interval and timeout are traceable to a measurement rather
;;                  than to a guess.
;;   :accepted      OPTIONAL. finding-key -> {:since :why :clears-when}, all
;;                  three non-empty strings, enforced by admission. A finding
;;                  listed here is CORRECT and deliberately not being fixed yet;
;;                  the tick reports it as ACCEPTED, never as NEW, and never
;;                  inside the STANDING count.
;;
;;                  It exists because the 2026-08-13 triage (ADR-2608132600)
;;                  needed a state the three classes could not express. Without
;;                  it, "we looked at this, we understand it, and the fix is a
;;                  decision someone else has to make" decays into STANDING --
;;                  the class this whole design calls indistinguishable from
;;                  silence. The decision would then be recorded nowhere, and a
;;                  month later would read exactly like a defect nobody opened.
;;
;;                  Two things keep it from becoming a way to make reds go away.
;;                  (1) Admission rejects an entry with no date, no reason or no
;;                  exit condition, so accepting costs as much writing as fixing
;;                  a small thing. (2) An acceptance whose finding has gone away
;;                  is reported LOUDLY as a stale acceptance, in the tick log and
;;                  at session start -- an exemption that outlives its subject
;;                  exempts whatever lands on that key next.
;;
;; ## Invariants this home holds itself to
;;
;;   1. It reads `orgs/` checkouts. It NEVER writes to one, never fetches, never
;;      checks out, never moves HEAD anywhere. Detectors are admitted on
;;      `:writes :none`, reviewed by a human before being added here, and are
;;      run with `GIT_ALLOW_PROTOCOL=none` so any `git fetch`/`clone`/`push`
;;      inside a detector fails at the transport layer rather than succeeding
;;      quietly. See the tick's header for what that env var does and does not
;;      cover, stated honestly.
;;   2. It writes NOTHING into any git checkout, including the superproject.
;;      State lives under `~/.itonami/orgs-detector-tick/`. A tick that dirtied the
;;      shared checkout would trip the stash-churn failure CLAUDE.md documents.
;;   3. It is a monitor, not a gate: exit 0 even with findings. The gate is the
;;      individual detector, run by a human or by a hook.
;;
;; ## What this home cannot host, stated rather than implied
;;
;;   - `annex-custody-verify` needs B2 credentials. It can run here (the keys
;;     are on this machine, not on a fleet node) but NOT under launchd: launchd
;;     cannot answer a Keychain unlock prompt, the wall recorded in
;;     ADR-2607178000. It belongs in the hand-run set, and putting it here on a
;;     schedule would produce a permanent :inconclusive -- another standing red.
;;   - Anything needing `gh` is rate-limited and shares one token with every
;;     concurrent agent session on this machine. Not scheduled here.
;;   - `audit-gftd-scripts` reads `$HOME/.gftd`, which is correct here and
;;     meaningless on a node -- but it is cheap and ADR-2608124800 ranks it as a
;;     SessionStart hook, which is a better fit than a 6-hour tick.
;;
;; ## The 2026-08-13 sweep of `scripts/verify-*.cljs`, and what it ruled out
;;
;; Thirteen scripts under `scripts/verify-*.cljs` mention `orgs/`. Four are
;; registered below. The other nine, with the class each falls in:
;;
;;   - `verify-duplicate-registrations` -- **`gh` class.** It calls
;;     `gh api repos/<slug>` once per candidate group to confirm two paths are
;;     one upstream by GitHub id. Rate-limited, one token shared with every
;;     concurrent agent session.
;;   - `verify-namespace-collisions` -- **not swept.** A sibling session was
;;     working on it on 2026-08-13; registering it concurrently would have
;;     pinned an argv that was being changed underneath.
;;   - `verify-repository-roles` -- **already hosted.** It is the fleet gate
;;     `root-repository-roles`, diagnosed and returned to green by
;;     ADR-2608124800. It needs no second home.
;;   - `verify-parity-probes` -- **already hosted, and it does not read
;;     `orgs/`.** It is the fleet gate `root-parity-probe-policy`; the string
;;     `orgs/` appears in it only inside a regex that matches the TEXT of a
;;     probe, and in the header prose. Its only input is
;;     `90-docs/maturity/engine-parity.datoms.edn`, which is in root's own tree.
;;   - `verify-no-babashka` -- **does not read `orgs/` either**, for the same
;;     reason: `--root orgs` appears in its usage comment, and its default root
;;     is `.`. ADR-2608124800 already classes it root-tree-only and gatable
;;     as-is, so what it is missing is a `gates.edn` line, not a registry entry.
;;     Measured here anyway, because pointing it at `orgs/` was the obvious
;;     thing to try: `--root orgs --allow-zero` ran for roughly 38 minutes on
;;     this machine without producing a line and was killed. It reads every file
;;     in the tree **in full** to test for a `bb` shebang, so its cost is
;;     dominated by the size of the artefacts it is not looking for.
;;   - `verify-clj-everywhere` -- **not a detector.** It is a test-suite
;;     orchestrator: four audits and then `clojure -M:test` in five repos. It
;;     emits no per-finding output to key on, it resolves Maven dependencies
;;     over HTTPS, and it writes to `~/.m2`, so `:writes :none` would be false.
;;   - `verify-kotoba-pages` -- **needs a real browser.** It drives Playwright
;;     against `file://` pages. CLAUDE.md's resource governor caps concurrent
;;     browser probes, and this machine's many parallel sessions already
;;     contend for OS focus; a browser launched unattended on a schedule is a
;;     materially different thing from a file-reading monitor.
;;   - `verify-vendored-copies`, `verify-require-graph` -- registered below
;;     since 2026-08-12.

{:schema 1

 :detectors
 [
  {:id       :verify-definition-repo-reachability
   :title    "a definition repository that nothing names, and why"
   :why      "2026-09-09: twenty-four kotoba.lang namespaces became one repository per
              definition (adr-2609091200), 452 of them. kotoba.coll spent part of a day
              exporting 7 of its 27 names because its assembled namespace was generated
              before the namespace grew and never regenerated -- all 31 definition repos
              existed the whole time and nothing said the namespace that assembles them
              was behind. A call site asking for kotoba.coll/postwalk got a symbol that
              did not resolve, which is silent until called. The useful question is not
              whether a repo is named but WHY it is not: four kinds of name are
              deliberately not carried (values, macros, protocols, record types), so
              without that separation the answer is nine repos and the real finding is
              one. Deliberately not a fleet gate: it reads every sibling under orgs/ to
              find out who names whom, and a gate ships one repo's tree."
   :argv     ["nbb" "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-definition-repo-reachability.cljs"
              "--findings" "{{root}}/orgs"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*"
   :interval-ms 86400000
   :timeout-ms  900000
   :writes   :none
   :measured "Landed 2026-09-09 with the finding path shown rather than asserted: a
              definition repository nothing names, planted under orgs/, produced exactly
              one FINDING naming it and moved SCANNED from 452 to 453; removing it
              returned UNREACHABLE 0. The FIRST control was wrong and stayed green --
              it renamed a function inside a repo the facade still named, which this
              detector does not measure -- so it proved nothing and was redone. Two
              defects were found while writing it, both of which reported a clean
              subset rather than an error: a lazy directory walk blew the stack on
              orgs/ and exited 0, and a name-escape that emitted two backslashes made
              the dead-code pattern match nothing, so a private helper nobody calls was
              reported as a missing public function. Its evidence floor is the
              namespaces the assembled namespaces REQUIRE, not west's registered
              checkouts: comparing against west counted 2,520 ordinary repositories
              that were never split and refused on all of them."}

  {:id :verify-wasm-toolchain-pin
   :title "the Wasm toolchain amu pins, against the versions the nodes actually have"
   :why "2026-08-20: amu's contract (kotoba-component's component-model-v1.edn,
              [:spec-baseline :wasi :toolchain]) pins {:wasm-tools \"1.243.0\"
              :wac-cli \"0.10.1\"} and refuses any other version before running.
              The fleet satisfies that from ~/.itonami/wasm-pin/bin, and the
              amu-jvm-test gate depends on it. When the contract moves, those
              binaries stay put and the gate goes red saying `wasm-tools version
              is not pinned` -- which reads as a repo failure and is not one. It
              is ADR-2608198600's shape (a node answering about itself, recorded
              as a verdict on the code), created by the change that added the
              gate. This says it in the right name, before the gate says it in
              the wrong one. Cannot be a fleet gate: the comparison needs
              kotoba-component's resource AND scripts/fleet-ci/nodes.edn, and no
              single shipped tree holds both. Measured while writing it -- the
              first version read the contract from amu, where it does not exist,
              and the refusal path is what caught that rather than a clean 0."
   :argv ["nbb" "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-wasm-toolchain-pin.cljs" "--findings" "--root" "{{root}}"]
   :cwd :root
   :findings :protocol
   :writes :none
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*"
   :timeout-ms 300000
   :interval-ms 43200000}
{:id :verify-fleet-malware-protection
   :title "the fleet's malware protection, measured rather than read off its settings"
   :why "2026-08-26, ADR-2608262300: having built a verdict plane whose whole point
              is that a scan which could not run must not return a clean scan's
              value, the same ADR admitted it had never measured what actually
              protects the fleet. Measured: all six reachable nodes had
              ConfigDataInstall=1, Gatekeeper enabled, SIP enabled and zero
              third-party agents -- every setting correct -- while their XProtect
              data sat at 5333..5346 and their last successful background update
              check was 87 to 167 days old. The operator workstation was at 5356,
              checked the previous day. A detector that reads the settings reports
              6/6 green; the settings are what is true, and they are not the
              question. Cannot be a fleet gate twice over: it needs ssh keys, which
              invariant 3 keeps off the nodes, and nodes.edn, which no shipped tree
              carries. The forced scan that fixed it took ten seconds per node and
              moved every one to 5356, so nothing was broken -- the scheduled scan
              simply had not run in three months, and only a measurement says so."
   :argv ["nbb" "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-fleet-malware-protection.cljs" "--findings" "--root" "{{root}}"]
   :cwd :root
   :findings :protocol
   :writes :none
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*"
   :measured "2026-08-26: 25s wall for 10 hosts (6 answered in ~1.5s each, 4 timed
              out at the 5s ConnectTimeout). Exit checked in all three directions:
              1 with findings, 2 when no node answers (prints SCANNED 0, which
              fails the evidence regex above and is recorded :inconclusive rather
              than clean), 2 when nodes.edn cannot be read. Remediation lives in
              the same script behind an explicit --remediate and is never in this
              argv."
   :timeout-ms 180000
   :interval-ms 43200000}
{:id       :compliance-scope-boundary
   :title    "certification scope drawn around domains, cut through by one Worker"
   :why      "2026-08-23, ADR-2608231500: asked whether SOC 2 could be obtained for
              five named domains, the honest first answer is that a SOC 2 report is
              issued to a legal entity over a system boundary, and a boundary can
              only be drawn where the control environment actually divides. The
              first run measured 8 Workers answering on more than one registrable
              domain, 5 of them touching a named property -- `net-kotobase` serves
              aozora.app + gftd.ai + kotobase.net from one config, one binding set
              and one deploy credential. Drawing a line around the five domains
              leaves five Workers straddling it. It also found one wrangler config
              that no parser accepts, so that Worker was absent from BOTH the asset
              inventory and the surface index while looking like it had simply not
              been deployed. This CANNOT be a fleet gate: a gate ships one repo's
              tree, and every input here lives under west-managed `orgs/`."
   :argv     ["nbb" "{{repo}}/scripts/gen-compliance-scope.cljs" "--findings"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*"
   :interval-ms 43200000
   :timeout-ms  600000
   :measured {:as-of "2026-08-23" :runtime-s 2 :load1 7.60
              :configs-listed 451 :configs-parsed 450 :findings 10}
   :writes   :none}

{:id       :dependency-vulnerabilities
   :title    "known vulnerabilities in the dependencies the audited surfaces actually ship"
   :why      "2026-08-23, ADR-2608231500: SOC 2 CC7.1 and ISO/IEC 27001:2022 A.8.8 ask
              whether known vulnerabilities are found and acted on. The MODEL for
              that has existed here for months -- `kotoba-lang/app-sbom` carries
              1,823 lines plus a vitest suite over component/purl, OSV CVE ingest,
              VulnMatch, severity-keyed SLA and blast radius -- but
              `sbom.etzhayyim.com` does not resolve and nothing had ever put this
              workspace's own dependencies into it. The first run measured 2,988
              dependencies across the 21 in-scope repos and found four vulnerable
              packages, one of them (undici 7.28.0, five advisories) shipping in
              eight repos including cloud-itonami-app and the kotobase.net control
              plane. This CANNOT be a fleet gate: it reads every in-scope checkout
              under west-managed `orgs/`, and it needs egress to api.osv.dev, which
              CLAUDE.md records as varying per node rather than being a constant."
   :argv     ["nbb" "{{repo}}/scripts/gen-dependency-inventory.cljs" "--osv" "--findings"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*"
   :interval-ms 86400000
   :timeout-ms  900000
   :measured {:as-of "2026-08-23" :runtime-s 40
              :repos 21 :dependencies 2988 :queried 956
              :skipped-range 55 :skipped-ecosystem 135 :vulnerable-packages 4}
   :writes   :none}

{:id       :verify-actor-metering
   :title    "governed actors that meter nothing, which is upstream of having no price"
   :why      "2026-08-31, ADR-2608314000: the offer ladder put all 31 governed actors
              in no-offer, and the reason a price book cannot be written for them is
              upstream of the price. `pricing/problems` refuses an invoice that
              quotes a dimension nobody counts, and ADR-2608110200 says why the
              order matters -- a price can be chosen later, a month that was not
              measured can never be measured afterwards. So the question is whether
              an actor records dimensioned usage at all, and that had been answered
              only by a substring search: 9 of 31 repos contain the word `usage` or
              `meter` somewhere in src, which is not evidence, because a comment
              matches too. This counts the shape instead, taken from authn.usage --
              the one meter in this workspace that actually supports a price book --
              which carries a period, a de-duplicating key, and an aggregate.
              Measured 31 of 31 with no metering namespace at all, so the 9 was
              indeed the wrong question.

              It reports meter-SHAPED, never metered. Whether a meter runs, or
              recorded last month, is not visible in a repository, and the reference
              itself says de-duplication is the part that cannot be reconstructed
              from a log afterwards. Collapsing those two words would let a
              namespace that has never executed be counted as measurement.

              Two conventions for actor.edn exist -- 24 bare `:did`, 6 ns-prefixed
              `:actor/did`, 1 nested -- and the first version of this detector read
              only the second, admitting 6 where itonami-actor-pnl admits 31. Two
              detectors answering one question with different populations is one of
              them being quietly narrower, so both are admitted and the split is
              reported."
   :argv ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
          "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-actor-metering.cljs" "--findings" "--root" "{{root}}"]
   :cwd :root
   :findings :protocol
   :writes   :none
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*"
   :timeout-ms 120000
   :interval-ms 86400000
   :measured "Landed 2026-08-31 in a documentation shape -- :run / :exits /
              :measured-as-a-map -- which admission refuses, because none of
              :argv :cwd :findings :evidence :timeout-ms :interval-ms was
              present. The whole registry was therefore exit 2 and the tick ran
              NOTHING, for every detector, not just this one. Rewritten into the
              runnable shape by measuring rather than guessing: run 2026-08-31,
              9.8s wall, exit 1, `SCANNED 32 actors` and 31 FINDING lines, and
              CONVENTIONS reported {:ns-prefixed 7 :bare 24 :nested 1}. The
              timeout is 12x that wall; daily because an actor gains a metering
              namespace on the scale of a landed ADR, not a commit. The prose
              :why above is the author's and is unchanged.
              Note the scanned count is 32 and the conventions are 7/24/1, where
              the entry as written said 31 and 6/24/1 -- the population moved
              between their run and this one, which is exactly why the number
              belongs in a measured field that gets re-measured and not in a
              claim."}

{:id       :verify-shadow-cljs-provider-paths
   :title    "builds that put a library on their source path but not that library's own dependency"
   :why      "2026-08-31: net-kotobase/control-plane's protocols-worker and
              network-awai/app-aozora-engine BOTH stopped at `The required namespace
              \"sha2.core\" is not available, it was required by \"kotobase/cid.cljs\"` --
              neither could be rebuilt from its own main, and both deploy live
              Workers. One library grew a dependency and its consumers' source paths
              did not follow. Nothing noticed because a build nobody ran is
              indistinguishable from a build that works. A sweep found the same shape
              in 8 more builds. This is deliberately not a fleet gate: it reads
              `orgs/` and it runs `git show <default>/main:<path>` per repo, and a
              gate ships one repo's tree with no siblings and no remotes."
   :argv     ["nbb" "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-shadow-cljs-provider-paths.cljs"
              "--findings" "{{root}}/orgs"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*"
   :interval-ms 43200000                 ; 12h -- source paths change slowly
   :timeout-ms  1800000                  ; 30m -- ~3x the measured 9m over 235 builds
   :writes   :none
   :measured "Landed 2026-08-31 with all three exit codes shown against a synthetic
              tree rather than asserted: a build missing the provider -> exit 1 and one
              FINDING naming the file and the namespace; the same tree with only the
              correct build -> exit 0 `clean`; a repo whose default branch cannot be
              resolved -> exit 2 and REFUSING, not clean. First live run: SCANNED 195
              of 235, 10 broken, 40 unreadable, so exit 2 -- the tree is NOT reported
              clean while 40 builds were never opened. It reads the DEFAULT BRANCH and
              not the working tree on purpose: a west checkout sits at its pin, and
              the first draft of this sweep read working trees and reported a repair
              that had already landed."}

  {:id       :verify-shared-library-consumption
   :title    "how each build reaches its shared library, and whether it can reach it at all"
   :why      "2026-08-31, root ADR-2608311600: kotobase.biscuit-grant -- the fleet's one
              Biscuit verifier -- moved out of net-kotobase/control-plane into
              kotoba-lang/grant. The API gateway followed via a pinned git dep. The
              protocols worker did not: its shadow-cljs source path still pointed at the
              emptied ../shared/src, its source still required the namespace, and MAIN
              COULD NOT COMPILE IT -- while the deployed artifact went on serving
              sparql, gremlin and graphql. A source path carries no pin and raises no
              error until somebody builds, so the gap between `deployed` and `buildable`
              stayed invisible. Three routes exist and only one is silent: a vendored
              copy is pinned and diffable, a git dep is pinned and fails loudly, a
              source path is neither. CANNOT be a fleet gate: it reads every repo and
              runs git against each one, and a gate ships a single tree."
   :argv     ["nbb" "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-shared-library-consumption.cljs"
              "--findings" "{{root}}/orgs"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*"
   :interval-ms 43200000                 ; 12h
   :timeout-ms  1200000                  ; 20m -- measured ~3m
   :writes   :none
   :measured "Landed 2026-08-31 after being wrong twice, both fixed rather than
              described. It first classified from WORKING TREES, so a west checkout at
              its pin made a correctly-vendored build read as `dangling` -- wrong in the
              direction that manufactures findings, measured on nexus-x402 the day its
              copy landed. It then folded exit status into an `:ok` meaning `0 or 1`,
              which is right for grep and wrong for git: `rev-parse --verify` failures
              read as successes, every repo resolved to the first remote in the list,
              and the correctly-pinned API gateway was reported as unable to compile.
              Classification now reads the default branch and reports exit status
              instead of interpreting it; rows carry how far the local checkout is
              behind, so a stale tree is legible rather than mistaken for a defect.
              First correct run: gateway pinned-dep, nexus-x402 vendored,
              protocols-worker DANGLING (真 -- fixed in control-plane 2a959b76),
              kotobase-worker-shell source-path and unpinned."}

  {:id       :verify-kotoba-stdlib-self-hosted
   :title    "kotoba stdlib repos that have gone back to a clojure.* namespace"
   :why      "2026-09-08: over three days the kotoba stdlib stopped delegating --
              clojure.string, clojure.set, clojure.walk and clojure.edn were
              replaced by kotoba.lang.text / .coll / .edn, the last of those a
              READER rather than a wrapper (ADR-2609040930 routing table). The
              value is not that names changed. It is that clojure.string and
              clojure.edn DO NOT MEAN THE SAME THING ON THEIR TWO HOSTS, and a
              delegating wrapper inherits every divergence silently. Measured,
              with the migration corpus as evidence: trim's whitespace class
              (the JVM excludes U+00A0/2007/202F and includes U+001C-001F,
              goog.string is the reverse); split with a capturing group
              (JVM drops the captures, JS String.split interleaves them, so the
              same .cljc file splits differently on the two runtimes); replace's
              $0 and \\$; 1N / 1.5M / 1/2 / 9e18; and :eof on the empty string.
              One reintroduced require puts a repo back on that footing and
              nothing says so.

              Three deliberate choices, each of which was wrong in a first
              version and is why this is not a grep. (1) The population is
              DERIVED from the tree -- a repo is in scope when its own src
              declares a kotoba.lang.* namespace -- because a hand-kept list
              goes stale silently. (2) It reads the default-branch remote ref
              and RESOLVES it rather than assuming origin/main: a west checkout
              names its remote after the ORG, and assuming origin/main hid FIVE
              still-unmigrated repos (checkpointer, mst-projector, pqh, process,
              witness-quorum) behind an honest-looking UNRESOLVED. CLAUDE.md
              records the same assumption breaking a deploy guard across 2,824
              of 4,406 checkouts, where it silently ALLOWED. (3) A repo whose
              ref cannot be resolved is UNRESOLVED, never clean -- that is how
              base-l2, whose checkout had no remote-tracking refs at all, was
              kept out of the migrated count until it really was migrated.

              clojure.java.io is reported at :info, not as a defect. Classpath
              lookup has no portable meaning -- there is no classpath on
              ClojureScript -- so it is a host mechanism, and ADR-2609040930's
              rule is to record such a dependency rather than invent a
              destination for it (its migration is adr-2809070100, still
              proposed)."
   :argv ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
          "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-kotoba-stdlib-self-hosted.cljs" "--findings" "--root" "{{root}}"]
   :cwd :root
   :findings :protocol
   :writes   :none
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*"
   :timeout-ms 180000
   :interval-ms 86400000
   :measured "Run 2026-09-08 from the orgs-bearing checkout: 39 repos in the
              derived population, exit 0, four :info findings and no defects.
              Wall time 23s, so the timeout is roughly 8x. Daily, because a repo
              gains or loses a require on the scale of a landed PR, not a
              commit. The first version of this entry ran with an assumed
              origin/main and reported SCANNED 33 with six repos UNRESOLVED;
              the numbers above are from the version that resolves the remote,
