  {:id       :verify-string-migration-hazards
   :title    "call sites where clojure.string -> kotoba.lang.text would change the ANSWER"
   :why      "2026-09-08: kotoba.lang.text stopped delegating to clojure.string and had
              to DECIDE three things clojure.string does not decide, because its two host
              implementations disagree and a delegating wrapper inherited the
              disagreement invisibly. kotoba.lang.text adopts the JVM's answer in all
              three and pins it.

              The workspace has ~3,900 repos and ~30,000 clojure.string call sites. The
              question a bulk migration has to answer first is not `how many` but `which
              ones would come out different`, and the honest answer turned out to be far
              smaller than the fear -- 3,002 split calls with a regex separator and 1,918
              replace calls with a regex match and a string replacement, of which:

                1 split has a capturing group, and it is .clj, so nothing changes
                8 replace use `$&`   (the whole match in JS; ordinary text on the JVM)
                6 replace use `\\$N` (an escaped dollar on the JVM; backslash + group in JS)

              Fifteen files. Nothing in the .cljc corpus is affected. Without this the
              migration either stops on a fear it cannot size, or proceeds and breaks
              fourteen regex-escaping helpers silently -- they would still run, and emit
              a literal `$&`.

              Two things it does deliberately. (1) The regex must be the SECOND ARGUMENT
              of split/replace, not merely somewhere on the line: matching the line
              reported 13 capturing-group splits where there is 1, because a `re-find`
              pattern elsewhere on the same line counted. (2) A paren inside a character
              class is LITERAL -- without that, `#\"\\s+[-|(]\\s*\" was reported as a capture.
              Both are discriminated by breaking them.

              trim's whitespace class is the fourth divergence and is NOT decidable from
              source -- it depends on whether the DATA contains U+00A0/2007/202F or
              U+001C-001F. 10,115 .cljc and 4,882 .cljs lines are in that population.
              Reported at :info rather than omitted, so the number is not mistaken for
              zero by anyone reading a clean run."
   :argv ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
          "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-string-migration-hazards.cljs" "--findings" "--root" "{{root}}"]
   :cwd :root
   :findings :protocol
   :writes   :none
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*"
   :timeout-ms 180000
   :interval-ms 86400000
   :measured "Run 2026-09-08 from the orgs-bearing checkout: 14.3s wall, exit 1,
              SCANNED 17,915 call-site lines, 15 warn findings (1 .clj capturing-group
              + 14 .cljs template) and 1 info. The timeout is ~12x the wall. Daily,
              because a new hazardous call site arrives on the scale of a landed PR.
              Discriminated twice: dropping the character-class exclusion takes the
              capturing-group count from 1 to 2 (the known false positive returns), and
              disabling the `$&` test takes those findings from 8 to 0."}

  {:id       :verify-credential-consumers
   :title    "a credential class that is minted, verifiable, and accepted by nobody"
   :why      "2026-08-31, ADR-2608311600: ADR-2608291200 minted a per-caller operator
              credential for api.murakumo.cloud (`mro_`, 15 minutes, scoped to named
              actions) and measured the whole passkey chain live against real services
              with a signing virtual authenticator. The consumer was never written.
              Measured across 4,329 checked-out repos: `/rpc/verify-operator` has ZERO
              callers outside the issuer's own repository, so the credential exists,
              verifies, expires and authorises nothing -- while the shared secret it was
              built to replace stayed the only key to /v1/kotobase/database-restore
              (one string, held by every operator process, compared with `=`, naming
              nobody in an audit, and unreadable by any human since 2026-08-29).
              Neither half was wrong; nothing asked whether they were connected, and a
              consumer that does not exist raises no error anywhere. CANNOT be a fleet
              gate: it must read the issuer in one repo and search every other repo,
              and a gate ships one repo's tree."
   :argv     ["nbb" "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-credential-consumers.cljs"
              "--findings" "{{root}}/orgs"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*"
   :interval-ms 43200000                 ; 12h -- credential classes are added rarely,
                                         ; and the walk is expensive
   :timeout-ms  1800000                  ; 30m -- ~5x the measured 5m24s
   :writes   :none
   :measured "Landed 2026-08-31 with all three exit codes shown against a synthetic
              tree: one endpoint consumed and one not -> exit 1 and exactly one FINDING
              naming the unconsumed one; adding the missing consumer -> exit 0 `clean`;
              removing the issuer -> exit 2 REFUSED, because an unread issuer must not
              be reported as an unconsumed credential.
              Two defects were found in this detector while writing it and both are
              fixed rather than described. It first ran three separate `grep -r` walks
              over 4,000 repos and did not finish in nine minutes; the cost is the walk,
              so it now walks once and buckets. It then excluded only the issuer's
              SOURCE FILE, which made /rpc/verify-operator look like it had three
              consumers -- its own ADR, its own README and its own compiled bundle --
              and reported `clean`. A consumer is somebody else, so the issuer's whole
              repository is excluded. That defect failed in the direction that hides
              the finding, which is why the synthetic tree above includes an
              issuer-local mention that must not count."}

  {:id       :verify-ipni-publisher-record
   :title    "the IPNI publisher record, against the identity that is actually publishing"
   :why      "2026-08-31, ADR-2608311800: manifest/ipni-publisher.edn named the
              2026-08-27 key while the live head had been signed by another since
              2026-08-30, and a fourth identity had never been written down at all --
              the file that exists to say which key publishes did not know which key
              was publishing. Its two retrieval candidates had also swapped: the host
              marked :ready 404'd all three CIDs reachable from the live head, and the
              host marked :broken served them and re-hashed correctly. Three of the
              four publisher identities still point retrieval at the 404ing host, and
              since every indexed row has Publisher.ID == AddrInfo.ID, each rotation
              minted a new PROVIDER identity too -- the file's own words are that an
              advertisement naming an address that cannot serve a verifiable block is
              worse than no advertisement. Nothing compared the record to the wire, so
              the record could only ever be as fresh as the last person who looked.
              CANNOT be a fleet gate twice over: it reads a superproject manifest AND
              it requires outbound HTTPS to cid.contact, and node egress is not
              uniform (measured 2026-08-13, 8 nodes 200 / zebulun 000)."
   ;; --classpath is for `ipni.metadata`, which reads the advertisement's
   ;; Metadata field. Required rather than reimplemented in the script: this
   ;; workspace has already paid for two implementations of that one wire field.
   :argv     ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}/orgs/kotoba-lang/io-ipni-specs/src"
              "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-ipni-publisher-record.cljs" "--findings"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*"
   :interval-ms 21600000                 ; 6h -- rotation is rare but a stale record
                                         ; is invisible, so do not let it run daily
   :timeout-ms  600000                   ; 10m -- measured ~40s, mostly cid.contact
   :writes   :none
   :measured "Landed 2026-08-31 with both directions shown rather than asserted.
              Clean run: exit 0, SCANNED 6, six checks ok, zero FINDING lines. Three
              controls, each naming the check it should: putting the stale peer-id
              back failed record-names-the-live-signer AND
              the-current-entry-is-the-declared-one; dropping the newest identity from
              :identity-history failed history-accounts-for-every-indexed-identity and
              named the missing id; claiming kotobase.net is :ready failed
              ready-candidate-serves-kotobase.net while the other candidate stayed ok.
              --selftest proves the peer-id and IPNS derivations reproduce a pair the
              manifest already held, and the CID constant was computed independently
              in python rather than by the code under test -- a derivation that cannot
              reproduce a known-good pair may not be used to judge a new one.

              2026-09-01: three checks added for :ipni.publisher/lifecycle-proof, so
              ADR-2608160300's success criterion stops being a sentence somebody wrote
              once. Clean run: exit 0, SCANNED 8, nine checks ok. An absence read off
              an instrument that cannot see a presence is not evidence, so the removal
              check refuses unless two controls hold first -- the witness multihash
              still returns its third parties, and the same matcher still finds this
              publisher at a CID it has not retracted. Without the second, a typo in a
              peer id reports a successful deletion. Four directions shown on doctored
              copies via IPNI_MANIFEST_PATH: unmodified exit 0; witness repointed at a
              still-advertised multihash exit 1, naming the peer ids still listed;
              control repointed at the retracted one exit 2 with the removal check
              `not asked: a control failed`; witness repointed at an unindexed
              multihash exit 2. The last of those did not work when first written --
              cid.contact answers 404, whose body does not parse, so `the index
              returns nobody` arrived as `could not be read` and the floor check could
              never fire for the reason it names. It now reads the status. --selftest
              anchors the lookup-key derivation on BYTES, not on the manifest it
              judges: sha256 of the 3 bytes 'Any' is the witness digest, and
              (drop (inc i)) -> (drop i) yields a visibly different key.

              2026-09-02: three checks added for :ipni.publisher/discovery-witness --
              the sample 90-docs/ipni_maturity/probe.cljs scores its heaviest axis
              (:discoverable-as-provider, weight 0.20) on. The probe used to carry that
              sample as a literal, and the literal was the CID the lifecycle proof
              RETRACTS, so the axis read 0 for eighteen hours while the hypothesis it
              stands for was green and passing the checks above -- and the loop kept
              ranking already-finished work first. A probe that carries its own sample
              is a probe whose sample nobody re-measures, so the sample moved into the
              record and is measured here every six hours. Clean run: exit 0, SCANNED
              9, twelve checks ok. Three directions shown on doctored copies via
              IPNI_MANIFEST_PATH: sample repointed at the retracted witness fails BOTH
              discovery-witness-is-not-the-lifecycle-witness and
              discovery-witness-still-advertised, each naming its own reason; sample
              repointed at a multihash the index does not associate with us fails only
              -still-advertised while -is-not-the-lifecycle-witness stays ok, so the two
              are independent; deleting the key fails discovery-witness-is-recorded.
              That last one exists because the audit averages over MEASURED axes only:
              an unrecorded sample makes the axis :unknown, and dropping a red axis
              RAISES the mean. Deleting the sample must not be a quiet way to score
              higher."}

  {:id       :verify-codec-seq-expansion
   :title    "base codecs that expand a byte into a seq per bit, whose constant factor never appears in the caller's own profile"
   :why      "2026-09-05 (ADR-2609051700): multiformats.base32/encode expanded every
              byte into eight lazy-seq elements, regrouped them with `partition 5`,
              and rebuilt each group with concat/repeat/count/reduce -- 288 seq
              elements and 58 intermediate collections for one 36-byte CID. Every
              DAG-CBOR link is a CID, so ipld/decode paid that once per link on its
              canonicality re-encode: 97 of 124 ms on a real 30 KB catalog-directory
              block holding 643 links. Downstream, api.murakumo.cloud's
              GET /infer/queue spent 760 ms of Worker CPU to return a 2-byte empty
              list, and that one route was 98.5% of the entire Cloudflare account's
              Worker compute -- 1.7 million CPU-seconds a week, about 2.8 cores held
              continuously. Nothing in the calling code was wrong, which is why it
              stood: the cost was two libraries down, the codec was correct, and
              every test passed. After the fix: encode 23.6x, ipld/decode 124 ms ->
              18.3 ms, production /infer/queue 760 ms -> 285 ms. The second rule
              covers the same day's other half -- ipld/decode answered `are these
              bytes equal` by building two 30,155-element PersistentVectors.
              This CANNOT be a fleet gate: it reads orgs/, which a gate never ships.
              It reports a SHAPE, never a timing -- the finding says `measure this`,
              and the authority on speed stays that repo's own bench."
   :argv     ["nbb" "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-codec-seq-expansion.cljs" "--findings" "{{root}}/orgs"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*"
   :interval-ms 86400000                 ; 24h -- codec primitives change rarely and
                                         ; the sweep is cheap; nothing here is urgent
                                         ; within a day
   :timeout-ms  600000                   ; 10m -- ~30x the measured 20s runtime
   :writes   :none
   :measured "2026-09-05: 20s over orgs/, 97 codec-shaped files scanned (checkouts
              only -- orgs/ is west-managed and an unchecked-out project is invisible
              to the filesystem, so SCANNED is not a workspace total and the detector
              prints NOTE saying so). 5 findings in 2 repos, both of them the
              libraries this detector was built from, whose shared checkouts still
              sit at the pre-fix pins. Both directions shown: run against the fixed
              io-multiformats worktree it exits 0 CLEAN, against the pre-fix one it
              exits 1 on base32.cljc; against the fixed io-ipld worktree core.cljc no
              longer matches while against the pre-fix one it does. Every run first
              proves it can still tell the two known-bad shapes from their
              replacements and REFUSES with exit 2 if it cannot -- exit 2 is also
              what an empty scan and an unreadable candidate return, so a run that
              could not answer never returns the value a clean run returns."}

