  {:id       :verify-char-int-coercion
   :title    "`int` applied to a character, in code ClojureScript runs, where that is not a code point"
   :why      "On the JVM a character is a Character and `(int \\a)` is 97. Under
              ClojureScript there is no character type: `(seq \"abc\")` yields
              one-character STRINGS and `int` of a string is 0. So `(mapv int
              \"Password\")` is the code points on one runtime and a vector of ZEROS on
              the other. It does not throw. It returns a vector of the right length and
              the right type, full of zeros, and every distinct input maps to the same
              output.
              Measured 2026-08-26, the idiom produced a wrong-and-plausible answer in
              three libraries in this workspace in ONE DAY, twice in code written after
              being bitten by it: Sec-WebSocket-Accept became a digest of zeros, two
              different passwords hashed identically, and a CRC catalogue check ran on
              nine zero bytes. All three were caught only because those libraries run
              their suite on both runtimes; a repository with a JVM-only suite has
              nothing that can see it.
              First scan of the whole workspace found 30 more across 28,008 .cljc/.cljs
              files, four of them in production source -- including a WAV header whose
              RIFF/WAVE/data magic went out as zero bytes, in a function whose own
              docstring says a wrong header yields `not an error but a transcript of
              noise`, and a role-seed that summed to zero for every role in a
              ClojureScript-only repository.
              High-confidence forms only. A bare `(map int x)` over a variable is NOT
              reported: deciding whether x holds a string needs type inference this does
              not have, and a detector that guesses trains people to ignore it. The
              lambda form counts parentheses rather than matching a pattern, because
              `(map #(bit-and (int %) 0xff) (.getBytes s \"UTF-8\"))` is correct code
              that a regex reads identically -- two real files here are that shape.
              Cannot be a fleet gate: it reads west-managed orgs/ checkouts, and no
              single repo tree carries them."
   :argv     ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
              "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-char-int-coercion.cljs" "--findings"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*\t"
   :interval-ms 86400000
   :timeout-ms  600000                  ; 10m -- 10x the measured 61s, all filesystem reads
   :writes   :none
