  {:id       :verify-dependency-substitution
   :title    "every external dependency kotoba-lang has, against the destination the ledger assigns it"
   :why      "`verify-jvm-dependency-surface` answers WHERE a JVM is still required -- a
              census of hosts. This answers the next question: for each external
              coordinate, WHAT is it supposed to become, and is that destination real.
              Neither subsumes the other.
              Measured 2026-08-26 across 2,131 present kotoba-lang checkouts, the whole
              external surface is 191 distinct coordinates. Most of it is already
              replaceable -- the foundational stdlib and the origin-plane mirrors cover
              the large majority by usage -- so the work those entries name is rewiring
              an import, not writing a library. Recording that is what stops the same
              libraries being proposed again.
              Two failure modes it exists to catch, both invisible by inspection:
              (a) a coordinate resolved by shipped code that manifest/dependency-
              substitution.edn does not name, which for JVM runtime deps the cutover
              contract calls forbidden and for npm is at minimum undiscussed; and
              (b) a :target the ledger names that west does not register -- a ledger
              pointing at a library nobody wrote is worse than no ledger, because it
              reads as though the migration were already possible.
              It also reports Node builtins declared as npm dependencies. Those do not
              resolve to the builtin: npm serves a real unrelated package for each name
              (fs@0.0.1-security is npm's reclaimed-squat placeholder, tls@0.0.1 a stub,
              crypto/net/path abandoned shims). Measured 2026-08-26, app-scap declared
              six at `latest`, which means whatever is published at install time.
              Both failure modes were shown to discriminate before landing: pointing a
              :target at a repository nobody wrote turns it red, and removing a
              coordinate the workspace actually uses turns it red.
              Cannot be a fleet gate: it reads west-managed orgs/ checkouts and
              manifest/west.yml together, and no single repo tree carries both."
