  {:id       :verify-cljs-runner-completeness
   :title    "ClojureScript test runners that run less than the suite they sit in"
   :why      "ADR-2608170200 moves :jvm-test fleet gates to :nbb-test, and the :nbb-test
              gate's own floor only requires that SOME `Ran N tests` line appear. A runner
              covering a third of its suite satisfies that floor, so a gate can be moved
              to a cheaper node and silently stop testing most of its subject with an
              identical signed receipt. Measured on kotoba-lang/columnar before its
              repair: `clojure -M:test` ran 36 tests / 117 assertions, `nbb test/run.cljs`
              ran 10 / 54, and with group/group deliberately broken the partial runner
              printed `Ran 10 tests ... 0 failures` and exited 0. Nothing prints the
              number it should have reached, which is why only a detector can see it.
              What it has already caught beyond columnar: kotoba-lang/kotobase-peer ships
              17 `_test.cljc` and 2 `_test.cljs`; its nbb runner auto-discovers
              `_test.cljc` only, so those two namespaces run under shadow-cljs
              (`:ns-regexp \"-test$\"`) and never under nbb -- while the runner's own
              docstring calls itself a genuine third platform tier. No other check in the
              workspace compares a runner against the tree it sits in."
   :argv     ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
              "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-cljs-runner-completeness.cljs" "--findings"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*\t"
   :interval-ms 86400000                 ; 24h
   :timeout-ms  600000                   ; 10m -- 18x the worst measured runtime
   :writes   :none
      :corrected "2026-08-24: it was reading PROSE as code. `names-mentioned` ran over the
              whole file, so a namespace explained in a `;;` comment or named in a
              docstring or a `println` counted as required -- and `runner-loaded-not-run`
              is exactly `mentioned AND NOT run`, so every one of its five findings was a
              runner being blamed for the paragraph explaining why it leaves a namespace
              out. `ongakuka/test/run_portable.cljs` never requires
              `ongakuka.catalog-test` at all; it spends fourteen lines saying why it
              cannot, and that was the evidence against it.

              Comments are stripped for every question; STRINGS are stripped only for
              `names-mentioned`. The two differ: what a runner RUNS can legitimately live
              in a string (etzhayyim/root's akashi runner builds its `run-tests`
              expression as one and hands it to a child process, and this file already
              records that reading only the reader-macro form reported all five of its
              genuinely-run namespaces as never run). What a runner REQUIRES cannot.

              The one-hop BFS through aggregator namespaces read unstripped text too, and
              had to be fixed separately -- the first pass looked correct on four repos
              and left langgraph reporting, which is how that second site was found.

              Effect: runner-loaded-not-run 5 -> 2, suite-uncovered 10 -> 13. The three
              that moved are genuinely covered by no runner, which is the true statement
              about them.

              Both directions on real data, not synthetic: with the mention only in a
              comment ongakuka reports suite-uncovered 1 / loaded-not-run 0; adding a real
              `(require '[ongakuka.catalog-test])` to the runner without running it puts
              it back to loaded-not-run 1.

              The two that remain are worth knowing apart. `langgraph` is the best version
              of this pattern in the workspace: `runner_coverage_test.cljc` holds an
              `excluded` map giving the reason AND a `:still-contains` substring that must
              still be present in the named source, so a stale exclusion fails the test.
              It is named in code and therefore still reported -- teaching this detector to
              recognise a declared, re-checked exclusion is the open item, not a fix to
              langgraph. `net-kotobase/control-plane` is unexamined."
   :declared-exclusions "2026-08-25: `runner-declared-exclusion` is a new, non-defect class.
              A namespace named as a key of a `def` whose name says it holds exclusions --
              `excluded`, `jvm-only` and the like -- IN the runner or in a file the runner
              RUNS is reported as declared rather than as an omission.

              Two repositories already did this and are the reason it exists:
                kotoba-lang/langgraph  runner_coverage_test.cljc binds `excluded` to
                                       {ns {:file .. :still-contains .. :because ..}} and
                                       ASSERTS the substring is still present in the named
                                       source, so an exclusion that stopped being true
                                       fails the test. This is the shape worth copying.
                kotoba-lang/aiueos     run-tests.cljs binds `jvm-only` to {ns reason} and
                                       refuses when an entry names a namespace that does
                                       not exist -- the floor that caught two stale entries
                                       on 2026-08-24.

              It recognises the DECLARATION, not the floor. A repository could bind
              `excluded` and check nothing, and this would be quieter for it. That is why
              the count is PRINTED in the summary rather than silently subtracted, and why
              the finding text names langgraph's `:still-contains` as the stronger form.
              What the declaration does buy: it is in code rather than prose, it lives in a
              file that executes, and it is somewhere a floor CAN be attached.

              Effect on the full sweep: runner-loaded-not-run 2 -> 1, plus one info. The
              one that remains -- net-kotobase/control-plane, kotobase-api-gateway-cljs, 1
              of 54 -- declares nothing, which is now the distinction the class draws.

              2026-08-25: extraction widened to every namespace-shaped token inside the
              form, not only `'`-prefixed ones. net-kotobase/control-plane writes
              `(def excluded '{kotobase.site-page-test \"reason\" ...})` -- the MAP is
              quoted, so no symbol inside carries a quote of its own, and reading only
              `'ns` missed the whole registry. That repository is the third to use this
              pattern and the strongest in a different way: a separate lint,
              `scripts/check-test-registry.cljs`, parses BOTH `test-namespaces` and
              `excluded` out of that one file and runs before the build, so the registry
              and the suite cannot drift apart. Strings are stripped first, so a reason
              that mentions a namespace cannot get in.

              With that, `runner-loaded-not-run` is 0 across the workspace -- and the
              whole arc is worth stating: the class began at 5 findings and not one was a
              repository defect. Three were the detector reading comments and docstrings
              as requires; two were deliberate, re-checked declarations it had no way to
              recognise.

              Discriminated on real data, both repositories: renaming langgraph's
              `excluded` to `off-the-runner`, or control-plane's to `left-out`, puts each
              straight back to runner-loaded-not-run 1."
:measured "2026-08-17 on this machine: 44s standalone (34s before it began reading
              package.json and deps.edn per project), 45s under the tick, over
              4,163 of 4,184 registered
              checkouts, 201 repos in scope (a ClojureScript runner AND at least one
              portable test), 225 runners, 366 oracle scripts counted and excluded, and
              2,034 repos with portable tests but NO runner counted separately -- never
              findings, since an absent runner is not an incomplete one, but on screen
              because 181 of 201 otherwise reads as a claim about the workspace when the
              real share of portable suites any ClojureScript host runs is 201 of 2,235.
              exit 0 in monitor mode with 21 findings over 20 repos; 181 of 201 in-scope
              repos complete. Ratchet verified against an isolated --state: first run
              recorded all 23, second reported none as NEW.
              `--strict` exits 1; a west.yml with no `path:` entries and
              one with paths but no checkouts both print `SCANNED 0` and exit **2**.
              All four finding paths were shown red AND green on real trees, and the
              headline case both ways on the SAME repo: columnar at 6e417bc reports 2 of
              3 uncovered, at 5e165f0 (the actual fix) it is complete.
              Calibration is the part worth trusting least on its face, so: the first
              version reported 779 findings and five successive measured corrections took
              it to 23. A `_test$` filename rule read 1,081 `test_*` files as runners;
              judging each runner separately called gftdcojp/tia's five deliberate
              subsystem runners defects; a repo-wide unit asked
              etzhayyim/root's akashi runner to cover all 1,033 tests in that monorepo;
              demanding an `(ns ...)` form declared 35 ordinary nbb scripts unmeasurable,
              one of which was complete; and reading only `'ns` and not `(quote ns)` made
              akashi's five genuinely-run namespaces look unrun. Each of those was found
              by opening the repo the finding named and comparing by hand.
              Two more corrections landed the same day, found the same way on
              kotoba-lang/nekko, which this script had reported as 16 of 17 uncovered:
              three of those sixteen were running the whole time. A test namespace ending
              in a bare top-level `(run-tests)` runs ITSELF, and nekko's `test:async` npm
              script is three separate `nbb -e \"(require '[ns])\"` invocations -- a
              runner declared in package.json, which a source-file-only audit cannot see.
              Both are read now; nekko reports 13, and those thirteen are genuinely
              JVM-only."
   ;; NO :accepted block. Keys are `<kind>:orgs/<org>/<repo>:<project>`, so no prefix
   ;; would match and it would be reported as a stale acceptance. The baseline gives the
   ;; wanted behaviour: a NEW finding means a runner just stopped covering its suite --
   ;; which, with gates moving to :nbb-test, is the moment coverage silently drops.
   }

  {:id       :verify-shadow-source-paths
   :title    "shadow-cljs builds that cannot compile, because a :source-path is missing"
   :why      "ADR-2608181700, 2026-08-18: kotoba-lang/kotobase-worker-shell and
              net-kotobase/cypher were both unable to compile at all -- each missing the
              same three :source-paths, each surfacing only after the previous was added.
              The shell's test suite had therefore NEVER executed; restoring the paths
              turned it on at 45 tests. Nothing was red, because `shadow-cljs compile`
              stops before a test runs and nothing was running that build. Both were
              found by hand, because they were the two repos that session happened to
              touch. This run finds 13 of 30 in the same state, including a third copy of
              that exact three-path drift in net-kotobase-enterprise-wave2."
   :argv     ["nbb" "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-shadow-source-paths.cljs" "{{root}}" "--findings"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*\t"
   :interval-ms 21600000                 ; 6h
   :timeout-ms  600000                   ; 10m -- 19x the measured runtime
   :writes   :none
   :measured "2026-08-18 on this machine: 31s wall, 198 shadow-cljs.edn found, 30
              examined (those declaring a relative sibling path), 17 ok, 12 UNRESOLVED,
              1 MISSING. Read-only: no git, no network, no compile.

              It does not compile. It resolves the closure the compiler would --
              seeded from the repo's own non-relative source-paths, walking requires
              through the siblings -- and reports namespaces nothing on the path
              provides. An unresolved name that EXISTS elsewhere in orgs/ is the drift
              and is a fail with the source-path to add; one that does not is reported
              as probably a :dependencies entry and never failed on, because otherwise
              every reagent build reads as broken.

              Two earlier versions of this script were wrong in opposite directions and
              both are worth remembering: a regex over the file reported 41 of 41 repos
              broken (it matched regex character classes, :refer names and the java.*
              imports in a .cljc :clj branch), and scanning every file in every sibling
              rather than the closure reported 37 of 41 (a library carries modules a
              given build never reaches)."}

  {:id       :verify-worker-name-collisions
   :title    "Worker names declared by more than one config, with different routes"
   :why      "2026-08-18: `kotobase-protocols-worker` is declared by FIVE wrangler
              configs with three different route sets over seven hostnames. One of them
              (net-kotobase-enterprise-wave2) lists `cypher.kotobase.net`, which is
              served today by the separate `net-kotobase-cypher` Worker. A Worker is
              addressed by name at publish time and there is no fast-forward check, so
              which hostnames it ends up serving depends on which config went last.
              This is the same shape as the 2026-07-25 incident where an older checkout
              republished kotobase.net and returned the signup funnel to 404 for eleven
              minutes. That one was same-routes-older-code; these ten are worse, because
              the hostname set itself changes.

              The enterprise-wave2 copy is currently harmless only because it cannot
              compile -- it is one of the 13 found by :verify-shadow-source-paths.
              REPAIRING ITS SOURCE-PATHS WOULD ARM IT. One detector prints a remedy the
              other says not to apply yet, which is why both belong in one report."
   :argv     ["nbb" "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-worker-name-collisions.cljs" "{{root}}" "--findings"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*\t"
   :interval-ms 43200000                 ; 12h -- config churn is slow
   :timeout-ms  300000                   ; 5m
   :writes   :none
   :measured "2026-08-18 on this machine: 892 wrangler configs found under orgs/, 890
              named, 263 names declared more than once. 253 of those have IDENTICAL
              route sets (duplicate checkouts; publishing a stale one ships older code
              to the same hostnames) and 10 have DIFFERENT ones. Only the 10 are failed
              on; the 253 are one aggregated warn, because 253 individual findings would
              bury them. Read-only, no network, ~10s.

              Any directory below orgs/ whose name begins with a dot is excluded: agent
              worktrees, pre-adoption backups and scratch run dirs all carry real
              wrangler files and counting them manufactures collisions nobody can act on.
              Enumerating them by name did not work -- the first version listed
              .claude/worktrees/ and .root-repository-adopt/ and still reported
              itonami-fleet-dispatch as a four-config collision, because two of the four
              were .cloud-itonami-app-adopt/ and .wt-run/. Measured: generalising removes
              exactly that one false positive and introduces none (10 -> 9)."}

  {:id       :verify-kip-quorum
   :title    "the signatures behind every KIP that has actually been admitted"
   :why      "A :final KIP is a decision this workspace has agreed to be bound by, and the
              only thing standing between `:status :final` in a text file and a decision
              nobody signed is this script checking the ed25519 quorum on it. Nothing ran it
              on a schedule: ADR-2608196100 counted it among twelve verifiers with no runner
              and no twin, and a check that never runs cannot notice a signature going bad,
              a signer being removed from the allowed set, or a KIP being flipped to :final
              by hand.
              It reads two west checkouts -- orgs/kotoba-lang/kip (the registry) and
              orgs/kotoba-lang/kagami (did:key resolution) -- so it cannot be a fleet gate:
              fleet ships one repo's tree to a node, and neither of those would be there.
              That is what puts it here rather than in scripts/fleet-ci/gates.edn."
   :argv     ["nbb" "--classpath"
              "{{root}}/orgs/kotoba-lang/kip/src:{{root}}/orgs/kotoba-lang/kagami/src:{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
              "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-kip-quorum.cljs"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "VERIFIED\t[1-9][0-9]*\t"
   :interval-ms 21600000                 ; 6h
   :timeout-ms  120000                   ; 2m -- 240x the measured runtime; the cost here is
                                         ; nbb startup, not the work
   :writes   :none
   :measured "2026-08-19 on this machine: 0.2s and 0.5s at load 20.2 for the registered form,
              0.2s twice for --all (which does the ed25519 verification). Three directions
              shown with the same script, all measured, not argued:
                --kips <tampered>  one hex digit flipped in a signature ->
                                   FINDING error quorum-not-met:kip-0001, exit 1
                --all              untampered -> VERIFIED 1, exit 0
                (registered form)  VERIFIED 0, exit 3
              **The third line is why the evidence key is VERIFIED and not SCANNED.** Before
              today this script exited 0 on an empty subject: two KIPs read, none :final, so
              nothing was checked -- and it said so in prose, to a human, while handing every
              machine a zero. SCANNED counts KIPs read (2); VERIFIED counts KIPs whose quorum
              was checked (0), and the registry's qualifier files a run with no evidence line
              as :inconclusive rather than clean. So this detector will read as INCONCLUSIVE
              until the first KIP goes :final, which is the true state of the registry and not
              a fault in the detector. Exit 3 is the 'could not answer' code ADR-2608136000
              asks for -- neither 0 nor 1."}


  {:id       :verify-app-content-address
   :title    "application manifests that identify their document by location, not by content"
   :why      "ADR-2608157000 decided an application's identity is the CID of its document,
              not the path it happens to sit at. 80 of 516 kotoba.app.edn manifests still
              identify by location only -- measured today, not asserted -- and nothing was
              watching whether that number moves in either direction.
              The judge is `content-address.core/addressed?`; this script deliberately carries
              no second copy of that decision. It reads two things under orgs/ (every app
              manifest, and the library that judges them), so it cannot be a fleet gate."
   :argv     ["nbb" "--classpath"
              "{{repo}}:{{root}}/orgs/kotoba-lang/content-address/src:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
              "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-app-content-address.cljs"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*"
   :interval-ms 21600000                 ; 6h
   :timeout-ms  600000                   ; 10m -- 70x the measured runtime, sized against the
                                         ; load this machine actually runs at, not the quiet number
   :writes   :none
   :measured "2026-08-19 on this machine, four directions:
                fixture: one addressed manifest   -> SCANNED 1,   0 findings, exit 0
                fixture: one located manifest     -> SCANNED 1,   1 finding,  exit 1
                the real orgs/ tree               -> SCANNED 516, 80 findings, exit 1, 8.5s at load 12.9
                library not checked out           -> exit 2 (refusal)
              Two defects were fixed to make this registrable, and both are this loop's
              recurring shape:
              (1) **It reported 80 violations to a human and zero to any machine.** The
                  located-only rows printed as prose (and only under --list); the registry
                  parses FINDING<TAB>severity<TAB>key<TAB>detail and nothing else. SCANNED
                  matched, no FINDING was seen, and the run would have been filed clean.
              (2) **Its refusal exited 1, not 2.** `set! exitCode 2` followed by a throw is
                  overridden by nbb's error handler, so 'could not answer' reached the caller
                  wearing the code for 'found violations'. It now exits without throwing.
              Everything else here was already careful -- three-valued exits, no mirrored
              judge, an explicit refusal when the library is absent, worktree copies excluded
              from the count. The gap was that its output was addressed to a reader.
              No root argument is passed: the script's default is the relative `orgs`, which
              keeps FINDING keys repo-relative and therefore stable across machines."}

