  {:id :verify-unbounded-query-paths
   :title "query paths that can read the whole graph, and hand-written full-plane scans"
   :why "`kotobase.server.datom-plan` has six rows and two bind no prefix -- `[_ _ o]`
              and `[_ _ _]` both become `:eavt []`, the whole graph filtered in memory.
              `kotobase.server.admission` refuses a query whose plan carries one, and
              that refusal only holds where it is CALLED.
              It was not called on the deployed SPARQL surface. `admission` landed
              2026-08-23 gating `do-q` and `run-indexed-compiled-graph-query`; the
              surface that actually answers `graph.sparql` is
              `kotobase.server.sparql-protocol/do-sparql`, which calls `ps/source-for`
              itself and went through neither. `SELECT * WHERE { ?s ?p ?o }` stayed
              executable from the network for the length of one merge. THIS DETECTOR
              IS WHAT FOUND IT, on its first run against real repositories, and the
              finding cleared on the next run once the gate landed -- both directions,
              on this workspace's own code rather than a fixture.
              The cost being refused is measured: `kotobase.query.bridge/materialize`,
              the other whole-database read, is linear in the documents while the query
              over the result stays near 1ms across a 64x range
              (90-docs/kotobase-performance/2026-08-23-bridge-materialize-scaling.edn)."
   :measured "2026-08-23 on this machine: 37,256 source files under orgs/ in 12s.
              Before the gate landed: 4 findings (2 ungated-source, 2 unbounded-scan).
              After: 2, both the plan tables named in :accepted below.
              A first, looser version matched the bare name `source-for` and reported
              htmldom, a bitcoin node and a character creator -- none of which have ever
              seen a datom. It now requires the `kotobase.server.pattern-source` REQUIRE
              as well, which is why the noise floor is 0."
   :argv ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
          "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-unbounded-query-paths.cljs"]
   :cwd :root
   :findings :protocol
   :writes :none
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*"
   :timeout-ms 600000
   :interval-ms 21600000
   :accepted
   {"unbounded-scan:orgs/kotoba-lang/kotobase-server/src/kotobase/server/datom_plan.cljc"
    {:since "2026-08-23"
     :why "This IS the planner. `{:index \"eavt\" :components []}` is the row the plan
           table documents as `full scan, as asked`, and something has to name it --
           the gate refuses the query before the plan is ever consulted, so the
           existence of the row here is not the hazard. The hazard the rule is for is
           a caller writing that map by hand somewhere else, going around both."
     :clears-when "the plan stops being able to express an unbounded read at all,
                   which would mean a store API that can bound a range read -- see the
                   same follow-up `kotobase.query.bridge/materialize` records for
                   `-list`."}
    "unbounded-scan:orgs/kotoba-lang/kotobase-client/src/kotobase/datom_plan.cljc"
    {:since "2026-08-23"
     :why "The client's copy of the same table, deliberately separate (its own docstring
           says why: forcing a graph-head runtime to upgrade an unrelated IPNS contract
           merely to obtain an index plan would invalidate its existing signatures).
           Same reasoning as the server copy."
     :clears-when "same as the server copy, or the two plans merge."}}}

  {:id :verify-silent-js-callbacks
   :title "letfn siblings handed to a JS callback by name, which nbb never invokes"
   :why "Measured 2026-08-19 (ADR-2608190100): `(.catch fetch-and-retry)` in
              kotobase-server did nothing under nbb/SCI -- no exception, no warning --
              so a retryable block-miss escaped as a generic error three layers away.
              Reduced to four lines, then narrowed in four directions: a single-binding
              letfn works, a name passed from the letfn BODY works, a sibling calling
              another directly works, and ONLY a name reaching a JS callback from inside
              a sibling binding fails. The detector encodes exactly that shape.
              The same trampoline is copied into six R2 workers. Those are shadow-cljs
              builds where passing by name works, so they are reported `med`: a hazard
              that becomes a defect the day the suite moves to nbb, which is the
              direction this workspace is moving. A repo that already ships an nbb
              runner gets `high` instead -- that is a fact on disk, not a judgement."
   :measured "2026-08-19 on this machine, four directions:
                self-test (10 fixtures, every one a shape actually run under nbb) -> 10/10, exit 0
                the real pre-fix file (kotobase-server 3d2523d trampoline)         -> 1 finding, exit 1
                the same file after the fix                                        -> 0 findings, exit 0
                the whole orgs/ tree                                               -> SCANNED 29423, 6 findings, exit 1, 75s
                                                                                     (+ SKIPPED-UNREGISTERED 253)
              Two defects were fixed before this could be registered, both of which made
              it answer confidently and wrongly:
              (1) It parsed its own argv as `(drop 3 …)`, which is only right when the
                  script is argv[2]. With a `--classpath` it took its own path as a root,
                  scanned ONE file, and reported findings in its own fixture strings.
              (2) Its first rule was `any letfn name used as a callback`, which is too
                  broad by three of the four measured shapes -- it flagged a production
                  `hydrate-db` that demonstrably builds its database correctly. Narrowing
                  took the count from 32 to 13 and the `high` count from 4 to 0.
              (3) It scanned unregistered checkouts under orgs/. One of them is the SAME
                  GitHub repo as a registered path under a renamed org, 324 commits behind,
                  and contributed three findings already fixed upstream -- ghosts in a tree
                  nothing ships from. Filtering by west registration took 13 to 6; the 253
                  skipped trees are printed, so the omission is visible."
   :argv ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
          "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-silent-js-callbacks.cljs" "--findings"]
   :cwd :root
   :findings :protocol
   :writes :none
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*"
   :timeout-ms 600000
   :interval-ms 21600000}

  {:id       :verify-dep-coordinate-identity
   :title    "one repository reachable under two dependency coordinates"
   :why      "tools.deps dedupes by the coordinate SYMBOL, so two symbols naming the same
              GitHub repository are two libraries to it: both land on the classpath, each at
              its own pin, and the same namespaces load twice from two revisions. Nothing
              errors. Measured 2026-08-20 across 4,460 checkouts -- 2,665 in-house git
              dependencies naming 421 repositories, of which 25 are reached under more than
              one coordinate. The two largest are kotoba-lang/langgraph (454 references, as
              both io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph and io.github.com-junkawasaki/langgraph-clj)
              and jp-go-digital-design-system (447, also as jp-go-dds).
              This is what the fleet's nbb-cross-runtime gate reports as its long CONFLICT:
              line, and it is one mechanism by which a repository stays pinned behind a fix
              that already exists -- two names are two pins to move, and moving one looks
              done. Four repositories were unblocked on 2026-08-20 by moving pins alone
              (nekko, tech-ipfs-specs-ipns, org-ietf-xdr, org-ietf-oncrpc), each having been
              recorded as blocked on an upstream defect that had already been fixed.
              Two shapes, both reported: rename-shaped, where the old repository name
              survives as a coordinate (compiler for amu, ed25519, mcp, svg), and
              group-shaped, where the group segment differs (kotoba-lang/css beside
              io.github.kotoba-lang/css, org.kotoba-lang/io-multiformats).
              2026-09-07: the rename-shaped half was only ever half-measured. Grouping
              was by the `:git/url` STRING, so it found an old name only while the entry
              still carried the OLD url -- the moment a consumer updates the url beside
              the coordinate, the pair goes invisible. Measured on a synthetic
              half-migrated tree (kotoba-kir beside osaho, each with its own url): this
              detector reported it CLEAN, which is the one case a rename most needs it
              for. It now groups by the repository's CURRENT GitHub name, resolved once
              per repository and cached; `--no-canon` keeps the old string grouping and
              still misses that tree, which is how both directions were shown. A
              repository that cannot be resolved makes the run UNANSWERED rather than
              clean. The second works,
              because a bare group forces an explicit :git/url, and is still a second
              identity for one library."
   :argv     ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
              "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-dep-coordinate-identity.cljs" "{{root}}"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*\t"
   :interval-ms 86400000                 ; 24h -- deps.edn moves in days
   :timeout-ms  300000                   ; 5m -- 200x the slower measured run
   :writes   :none
   :measured "2026-08-20 on this machine, twice: 1.37s and 0.98s at load 5.9, identical
              output both times -- SCANNED 2665, CENSUS repos=421 multi-coordinate=25,
              26 findings, exit 1. It reads deps.edn only, so it is far cheaper than the
              detectors that walk trees.
              Both directions observed before landing: a synthetic tree with one repository
              under one coordinate exits 0; adding a second checkout that names the same
              repository as `kotoba-lang/css` with an explicit :git/url -- the exact shape
              found in the real tree -- exits 1 with the finding. A tree with no orgs/ and a
              tree of checkouts with no deps.edn both exit 2.
              The 26th finding is not a coordinate: orgs/etzhayyim/com-etzhayyim-isic's
              deps.edn does not parse (`Unmatched delimiter }`). An unparseable file is
              reported rather than counted as having no dependencies, because the second
              would turn a broken file into a clean one.
              `{{root}}` is passed explicitly: the script defaults to `.`, and a tick running
              from a worktree would otherwise find no orgs/ and refuse.
              ⚠ 2026-08-20, same day: its first version took only `:git/sha` deps and therefore
              could not see the larger half. A `:local/root` entry names a repository too --
              by directory rather than by URL -- and the workspace is full of coordinates that
              name one repository with a path resolving to another
              (a coordinate saying langchain-clj with a :local/root path resolving to
              kotoba-lang/langchain).
              Counting both took the population from 2,665 deps / 421 repos / 18 multi-coordinate
              to 5,642 / 568 / 45. A conclusion drawn from the first number was measuring
              less than half of what exists.
              ⚠ It reads WORKING TREES. Its first run reported com-etzhayyim-isic's deps.edn
              as unparseable and it was -- in a checkout four weeks behind, whose main had
              already fixed it. Checkout, west pin and the repository's main are three
              different things (ADR-2608136800); check main before acting on a finding."}

  {:id       :verify-kotoba-source-census
   :title    ".kotoba files that are not Kotoba source, and repos holding both under one extension"
   :why      "Measured 2026-08-19: 2,044 tracked `.kotoba` files, of which 988 are a legacy
              C-shaped schema DSL generated by deepen_actors.py and 1,019 are Kotoba source.
              The extension does not separate them, so every count, grep, build glob and
              migration report that keys on it conflates a language corpus with a data corpus
              of almost the same size. ADR-2608191000 measured the first census by hand after
              a status question was nearly answered with the bare total.
              kotoba-lang's lang/q9-migration.edn named this in July
              (:legacy-schema-dsl-extension-collision, 853 of 1,052 then) and stated the rule
              that only Clojure-shaped Kotoba source may use the bare extension. Nothing has
              ever measured it, so the violation has been standing and invisible.
              What it adds beyond the count: the four repositories that hold MORE THAN ONE
              shape, where no per-repo glob can separate them, and the 37 files that are
              neither source nor the known DSL (25 EDN data, 7 unrecognised, 5 tracked by
              git and absent from the working tree).
              That last class was called `empty-or-comment-only` when this entry landed,
              and every one of the five was a file the detector could not READ -- it
              caught the exception and passed \"\" to the classifier, which measured the
              absence as emptiness. Fixed same-day: `:unreadable` is its own class, and a
              tree where every listed file is unreadable now exits 2 instead of reporting
              a census of nothing."
   :argv     ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
              "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-kotoba-source-census.cljs" "{{root}}"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*\t"
   :interval-ms 86400000                 ; 24h -- the corpus moves in days, not hours
   :timeout-ms  600000                   ; 10m -- 18x the slower of two measured runs
   :writes   :none
   :measured "2026-08-19 on this machine, both directions actually observed before landing:
                real tree, run 1        15.3s at load 5.4  -> SCANNED 2044, 42 findings, exit 1
                real tree, run 2        33.2s at load 9.4  -> identical census, exit 1
                synthetic tree, 1 source file             -> exit 0, `OK - all 1 ...`
                synthetic tree + 1 legacy DSL file        -> exit 1, collision + mixed findings
                tree with no orgs/                        -> exit 2, UNANSWERED
                tree with checkouts but no .kotoba        -> exit 2, UNANSWERED
              The 2x spread between the two real runs is load, not variance in the answer:
              the census and the finding count were byte-identical.
              Three defects were fixed before registering, each of which made it answer
              wrongly rather than loudly:
              (1) argv was parsed as `(drop 3 …)`, so with a --classpath it took the
                  classpath as the root and refused with `no orgs/ under .:scripts/nbb_compat`.
                  The refusal was correct; the parse was not. Now `*command-line-args*`.
              (2) BSD xargs caps `-I` replacement at 255 bytes and this pipeline substitutes
                  {} twice, so a long checkout path died with `command line cannot be
                  assembled, too long`. It passed on the real tree, whose paths are short,
                  and failed only on the synthetic tree in the scratchpad. `-S 65536` fixes it.
                  Had the both-directions proof been skipped, this would have shipped.
              (3) That crash exited 1 -- indistinguishable from `the workspace has defects`.
                  The enumeration is now wrapped and exits 2, the class ADR-2608136000 names.
              `{{root}}` is passed explicitly: the script's default root is `.`, and a tick
              running from a worktree would otherwise census a tree with no orgs/ and refuse."}

