              一度も赤くならない gate も同じだけ無内容』）。"}

  {:id       :verify-negative-zero-literals
   :title    "-0.0 written as a literal, on the runtime where that stopped meaning -0.0"
   :argv     ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
              "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-negative-zero-literals.cljs" "--findings" "--root" "{{root}}"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*\ttracked .cljc/.cljs"
   :interval-ms 86400000                 ; 24h -- a source shape, it moves when someone writes code
   :timeout-ms  900000                   ; 15m -- measured 395s at load 5.17; 4,284 `git ls-files` calls dominate
   :writes   :none
   :why      "Measured 2026-08-24, the same expression on three nbb versions:
              ((fn [] (/ 1.0 -0.0)))  ->  1.4.208 ##-Inf / 1.4.210 ##-Inf / 1.5.212 ##Inf.

              A -0.0 literal loses its sign under SCI on nbb 1.5.212 when the analyzer
              treats it as an EXPRESSION inside a function body. Characterised exactly
              (this entry first said `inside a function body`, which was too broad and
              was wrong about two of its own nine findings):

                loses the sign  direct argument to a call, let binding value, returned
                                bare, argument to (list ...), #() body
                keeps the sign  inside a [...] or {...} data literal, and anything
                                COMPUTED -- (- 0.0), (* -1.0 0.0)
                top level       every shape keeps its sign

              That split cannot be decided by scanning: (let [z -0.0] ...) has [ as its
              innermost delimiter and loses the sign, while [1.0 -0.0] has the same
              delimiter and does not. So the detector reports every code-position
              literal and does NOT claim to know which are live -- it prints a RUNTIME
              line measured by evaluating both shapes in the interpreter running it.
              A later nbb that fixes this will say so on its own rather than have the
              detector repeat a stale measurement.

              1.4.208 and 1.4.210 get every shape right, and the JVM always has, so
              this is a regression between 1.4.210 and 1.5.212. The workspace was blind
              to it in the worst possible way:
              scripts/fleet-ci/gates/nbb-cross-runtime.cljs PINS 1.4.210 (correctly --
              1.4.208 cannot load a deftype with a custom protocol), so the gate is
              green on the version nobody installs and silent about the one they do.

              It had two live victims the day it was found, in repositories that had no
              idea they shared a bug -- kotoba-lang/io-ipld test/ipld/value_test.cljc
              and kotoba-lang/kotobase-engine-contract
              test/kotobase/engine/canonical_test.cljc. Both wrote #(f -0.0) to assert
              a codec REJECTS negative zero; on current nbb the thunk passed +0.0, so
              the assertion tested nothing it meant to. Both are fixed (io-ipld
              42985bc, engine-contract adbdaae) and both are clean here now.

              Cannot be a fleet gate: it reads every checkout under orgs/, and the
              fleet ships one repo's tree (the shape root-permit-index stayed red on
              for 300 runs).

              Reports every code-position occurrence, not only the ones in function
              bodies. A top-level (def z -0.0) reads correctly today and stops the
              moment someone inlines it -- an edit that looks like a refactor, not a
              value change -- and (- 0.0) costs nothing, so an exception would only be
              something to forget. Findings name the paren depth so a reader can tell a
              live defect from a latent one without opening the file."
   :measured "2026-08-24 on this machine at load 5.17: 395s, exit 1,
              SCANNED 28233 tracked .cljc/.cljs across 4284 checkouts, self-check 10/10.
              9 code-position literals in 8 files (hakoniwa, actor-shiori,
              com-etzhayyim-shiori, bim x2, kami-app-car-sim, org-ietf-jcs,
              org-vrmc-vrm, rdf-canon). Two of those nine are NOT live on any nbb --
              org-ietf-jcs is a top-level call argument and rdf-canon sits in a vector
              literal -- which is how the over-broad first wording was caught: the
              claim was checked against its own findings. An earlier plain grep
              returned 27 lines; the other 18 are docstrings and ;; comments, four of
              them in io-ipld's own value.cljc explaining this very rule. That gap is
              why this reads with reader state instead of a regex.

              Both directions, on synthetic AND on real data:
                self-check, 10 cases, run before it is allowed to say anything about
                  the workspace -- bare literal in a call (1), top-level def (1),
                  inside a ;; comment (0), inside a docstring (0), escaped quote in a
                  string (0), character-literal semicolon then a real one (1), -0.05
                  (0), x-0.0 (0), (- 0.0) (0), and -0. / -0.000 together (2).
                real: --repo orgs/kotoba-lang/io-ipld at its fixed tip -> exit 0, OK.
                  Reverting ONLY that one expression in the working tree -> exit 1,
                  naming test/ipld/value_test.cljc:146 at depth 7. Restored, clean.
                  Run under 1.4.208, 1.4.210 and 1.5.212: same finding, and the RUNTIME
                  line correctly says LOSES-SIGN for the third and correct for the
                  other two.

              The RUNTIME version comes from nbb.core/version, the interpreter running
              the file, NOT from shelling out to `nbb --version`. The first version of
              that function did shell out and printed
                RUNTIME nbb v1.4.208  expression-in-fn-body=LOSES-SIGN
              which is false about 1.4.208 and true about the nbb that ran it -- a
              version that does not belong to the measurement beside it, which is the
              same defect the detector exists to find.

              The self-check is not decoration. The first scanner written here called
              subs with one index, which throws -- and a detector that dies before
              printing SCANNED looks exactly like one that found nothing, to a caller
              reading only the exit code.

              What it does NOT see: .clj (JVM-only, where the literal is correct), and
              the OTHER 1.4.210->1.5.212 regression found the same day (amu's ir/lower
              overflowing the SCI stack on a 243-byte fixture, ADR-2608242100), which
              is not a source shape. This closes one class; the version-divergence
              question is still open."}

  {:id       :verify-grammar-lowering-drift
   :title    "forms the grammar authority declares admitted, against what the compiler can lower"
   :argv     ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
              "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-grammar-lowering-drift.cljs" "--findings" "--root" "{{root}}"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*\tprobed forms"
   :interval-ms 86400000                 ; 24h -- it moves when the grammar or the compiler does
   :timeout-ms  600000                   ; 10m -- measured 35s at load 4.14, 17x
   :writes   :none
   :why      "kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang's lang/guest-grammar.edn is the authority for the
              source surface, and root ADR-2607279200 Delivery #1 says in as many words
              that what compiler/frontend.cljc accepts and what the authority admits are
              different things whose drift is to be closed with a machine check. Measured
              Nine forms it declares for all three backends are rejected by the compiler
              with `operation has no admitted lowering` --
              conj contains? count dissoc keys peek str subs vals.
              (2026-08-24 this entry said eleven, including `disj` and `set-literal`.
              A keyword set lowers; the probe that said otherwise changed two forms at
              once and used an element type the surface rejects first. `disj` fails with
              `no admitted TYPE SIGNATURE`, a stated reason rather than a missing
              lowering. See the corpus rules in the script.)

              Cannot be a fleet gate: the authority lives in one repository and the
              compiler in another, and the fleet ships one repo's tree. Same shape as
              verify-wasm-toolchain-pin.

              Every probe is PAIRED with a control -- the same program with the form under
              test replaced by one known to lower. That is the whole design, because a
              probe written wrong is rejected too and a naive detector calls that drift.
              Not hypothetical: the hand pass that preceded this (ADR-2608243300 as first
              written) reported nine, and the paired version shows it was wrong in BOTH
              directions -- six of those nine had no control, and conj and peek were
              dismissed as shape problems while being real. The corrected list is eleven,
              and the ADR now says so.

              Coverage is printed on every run and is the reason the number can be read at
              all: 25 of 73 declared forms are probed. An unprobed form is UNMEASURED, not
              clean."
   :measured "2026-08-24 on this machine at load 4.14: 35s, exit 1,
              SCANNED 25 probed forms of 73 declared, controls lowered 25/25,
              9 drift / 2 rejected-with-a-reason (disj, take) / 0 unverified.

              Three floors, and each was reached during development rather than reasoned
              about:
                - No control lowered -> exit 2, `this harness is broken, not the compiler`.
                  The first version passed the probe source as a trailing argument to
                  `nbb -e`, which nbb reads as a script path, so every control failed. The
                  floor refused to report 20 harness errors as 20 drifts.
                - A control that does not lower -> that form is UNVERIFIED, never drift.
                  Six map probes landed here first (the parameter type was
                  `[:map :keyword :i64]` where `:map` is what a map literal satisfies), and
                  that is how the shape error was found rather than published.
                - Coverage printed every run.

              Discriminated: replacing `count`'s control with a call to a nonexistent
              operation moves it out of the drift list and into UNVERIFIED, naming the
              reason -- 11 drift becomes 10 drift plus 1 unverified."}

  {:id       :verify-cljs-runner-exit-signal
   :title    "ClojureScript test runners whose failure signal cannot reach the caller"
   :argv     ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
              "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-cljs-runner-exit-signal.cljs" "--findings" "--root" "{{root}}"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "CHECKABLE\t[1-9][0-9]*"
   :interval-ms 86400000                 ; 24h -- moves only when a runner is edited
   :timeout-ms  300000                   ; 5m -- measured 12s; it reads text, it runs nothing
   :writes   :none
   :why      "Two families of runner in this workspace call themselves the PRIMARY
              automated quality gate for their actor, signal failure by setting
              js/process.exitCode from :end-run-tests, and document their invocation as
              `cljs.main --target node -m <the-runner-ns>`. Those two do not compose.
              That form evaluates -main inside a node REPL environment; the process a
              caller waits on is the driver, and it exits 0 whatever the tests did. The
              exitCode never reaches it, and js/process.exit hangs the driver instead of
              propagating. So the gate prints `1 failures` and returns success -- the
              ADR-2608136000 shape at its purest, a check that could not report failure
              returning the same value as one that passed.

              Only files that BOTH set the exit code and document an invocation are
              checkable from the file alone; the rest are invoked from a gate or a
              package.json script, and under nbb the exit code works. Those are printed
              as UNMEASURED, never as clean. The first pass at this detector did call
              them findings and produced 380 of them, none a defect."
   :measured "2026-08-25 on this machine: 12s, exit 0, SCANNED 7725 test sources,
              CLAIMS-SIGNAL 407, CHECKABLE 33, FINDINGS 0 -- the whole population was
              fixed the same day. It went 33 -> 32 -> 0, and the middle number is the
              one that matters: 32 was measured after ONE repo had been converted by
              hand, so this check has shown both of its answers against real code and
              not only against a mutation.

              The underlying claim was measured dynamically in cloud-itonami-isic-6492
              before this detector was written, in both directions:
                -m form,  one assertion broken   -> `1 failures`, exit 0
                -m form,  unmodified             -> `0 failures`, exit 0
                compiled, one assertion broken
                          inside an async callback
                          (lands after run-tests
                          has already returned)  -> `1 failures`, exit 1
                compiled, unmodified             -> `0 failures`, exit 0

              Discriminated on a REAL fix rather than a mutation: isic-6492's runner was
              converted to the compiled invocation and given *main-cli-fn*, and this
              detector went 33 -> 32 with that repo the one that left. The same run
              therefore carries both a positive and a negative instance.

              A third measurement is why the detector also has a
              :compiled-without-main-cli-fn class: a compiled bundle whose namespace does
              not set *main-cli-fn* loads every namespace, runs no test, and exits 0 --
              indistinguishable from a clean run in both the output and the exit code."}

  {:id       :verify-cljs-shift-width
   :title    "bit shifts of 32 or more in code ClojureScript runs, where the count wraps"
   :argv     ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
              "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-cljs-shift-width.cljs" "--findings" "--root" "{{root}}"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SHIFTS-READ\t[1-9][0-9]*"
   :interval-ms 86400000                 ; 24h
   :timeout-ms  600000                   ; 10m -- measured 2m11s over 28,581 files
   :writes   :none
   :why      "JavaScript takes shift counts modulo 32 and cljs bit-shift-left /
              bit-shift-right / unsigned-bit-shift-right compile straight to them, so
              `(bit-shift-left 1 32)` is 1 and `(unsigned-bit-shift-right x 32)` is the
              low word. It does not throw; it returns a plausible number of the right
              type. On the JVM the same expression is right, so a .cljc file is correct
              in the suite that runs and wrong in the runtime nobody asked.

              Found by hand three times in two days before this existed: cljs-i64/ashr
              (divisor 2^shift, wrong for every shift >= 32), pe32plus/read-le (ELF
              64-bit fields with the high four bytes folded into the low bits -- an
              admission check that failed OPEN on the second runtime), and
              object.pe32plus/little-endian (PE image-base at width 8, bytes 4..7
              repeating 0..3). Two of the three sat in files whose own comments already
              explained this exact hazard, in a different function."
   :measured "2026-08-25 on this machine: 2m11s, exit 1, SCANNED 28584 .cljc/.cljs files,
              SHIFTS-READ 1596, SHIFTS-COMPUTED 708, SHIFT-LEFT-31 2, FINDINGS 12.
              It opened at 23 findings across 14 files in 10 repositories the same day
              and has been coming down as they are fixed.

              SHIFT-LEFT-31 is a SEPARATE, non-failing count, and the reason is a
              measurement. A left shift by exactly 31 is negative on ClojureScript and
              2^31 on the JVM -- a real divergence -- but it is usually deliberate: code
              that builds a 32-bit word and masks it, writes it into a Uint32Array, or
              runs it through its own `u32` helper is correct as written. Three sites
              existed when the rule was added: one real (opus.celt's overflow guard
              compared against a negative number, so the bits->pulses loop returned on
              its first iteration and every band got zero pulses) and two deliberate.
              One in three is not a finding rate, so it reports and never fails.

              Cross-checked against an independent Python implementation of the same
              scan written first: identical 23-element finding set.

              `#?(:clj ...)` branches are blanked IN PLACE rather than deleted, so a
              reported line number is the one a reader opening the file sees. The first
              version deleted them and a spot check landed five lines away, reading as a
              false positive -- which is how the kotoba.map/mvt finding was nearly
              discarded. It is a real defect in a `:cljs` branch: `f64-bits->double`
              computes its high word as `(unsigned-bit-shift-right bits 32)`, which on
              ClojureScript is `bits >>> 0`, so the high word equals the low word.

              SHIFTS-COMPUTED is printed every run and is UNMEASURED, never clean: a
              count like `(* 8 index)` cannot be classified by reading, and two of the
              three motivating defects were exactly that shape."}

  {:id       :verify-cljs-inexact-integers
   :title    "integer literals ClojureScript cannot represent, in code it executes"
   :argv     ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
              "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-cljs-inexact-integers.cljs" "--findings" "--root" "{{root}}"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "LITERALS-EXAMINED\t[1-9][0-9]*"
   :interval-ms 86400000                 ; 24h
   :timeout-ms  600000                   ; 10m -- measured 2m over 28,586 files
   :writes   :none
   :why      "ClojureScript has no 64-bit integer; every number is an IEEE-754 double, so
              a literal needing more than 53 bits of significand is rounded the moment it
              is read. On the JVM the same literal is an exact long.

              The recurring shape is a 64-bit PRNG transcribed into .cljc:
              `(unchecked-add (unchecked-multiply state 6364136223846793005) ...)`.
              Measured 2026-08-25 under nbb, one step from state 12345 gives
              7.856670437842954e+22 -- a float, with no 64-bit wraparound anywhere; the
              JVM gives -1206486762903477482. `unchecked-multiply` exists in cljs.core
              and does not wrap.

              This is a separate question from verify-cljs-shift-width, not a footnote on
              it. That detector flags `(bit-shift-left 1 53)` inside these same PRNG
              functions, and fixing that shift alone would make the files LOOK portable
              while the multiply above it still never wrapped."
   :measured "2026-08-25 on this machine: 2m, exit 1, SCANNED 28586 .cljc/.cljs files,
              LITERALS-EXAMINED 132, FINDINGS 36 across 23 files.

              Cross-checked against an independent Python implementation written first:
              identical 36-element set, matching on file, line and literal.

              The test is EXACT REPRESENTABILITY, not magnitude. A first version compared
              against 2^53 and reported 1000000000000000000 and -9223372036854775808 --
              both far past 2^53 and both exact (10^18 has a 42-bit significand; -2^63 is
              a power of two) -- while 9223372036854775807, of the same size, is not. The
              check now round-trips each literal through a double.

              Discriminated on a fixture carrying all four cases plus one inside
              `#?(:clj ...)`: only the inexact one is reported, and the :clj literal is
              not even counted as examined.

              One finding is deliberate: org-ietf-xdr's core_test.cljc writes 2^53 + 1
              inside a :cljs-only deftest asserting that ClojureScript has already
              rounded it and that encoding it must be refused. Named in the script so
              nobody corrects a correct test."}

  {:id       :verify-cljc-runtime-parity
   :title    "portable .cljc that manipulates bytes and has never been run on ClojureScript"
   :why      "2026-08-30. Over two days this workspace shipped 26 protocol codecs and hit
              the SAME bug class eight times: `.cljc` byte code that passes on the JVM and
              is silently wrong under ClojureScript. org-opcfoundation-ua's decode-int32
              leaned on JS bit-or sign-extension; org-iso-8583's `(int c)` on a character
              is a code point on JVM and a numeric-string parse in cljs, so a non-digit
              became 0 and \"02A0\" passed as a valid MTI; org-ietf-diameter's
              bytes->uint32 went NEGATIVE for any Application-Id above 0x7fffffff;
              org-can-cia-canopen, org-ethercat and com-profibus-profinet each had the
              identical u32 sign bug; org-lora-alliance-lorawan lost precision on 64-bit
              DevEUI magnitudes; and org-amqp encoded the \"AMQP\" magic bytes as ZEROS in
              PRODUCTION code.
              Every one was caught only because the suite was run on both runtimes. Every
              one was green on the JVM.
              org-modbus's README names this trap explicitly, and the second wave walked
              into it anyway -- which is the same shape as this workspace's standing
              finding that a docstring is not read by the execution producing the wrong
              answer. Prose did not prevent it. That is why this is a detector.
              WHAT IT MEASURES: not the bug (a pattern rule for it is mostly false
              positives -- `(int x)` on a number is fine). It measures the structural
              precondition: a repo whose source is `.cljc` is CLAIMING it runs on both
              runtimes; does it ever actually run on cljs? Signal A is error only when the
              repo also does bit/byte work, because that is the population the eight
              incidents came from. The rest is reported :info -- the portability claim is
              still unverified there, but those repos cannot carry this bug class, and
              burying 318 repos that can inside 2180 that mostly cannot is how a detector
              gets ignored.
              Signal B (risky constructs) is :warn and explicitly NOT presented as defects
              -- measured false-positive rate about 1 in 3 on a hand-read sample, mostly
              reader-conditional-guarded `mapv int` pairs the regex only half sees.
              It cannot be a fleet gate: it reads orgs/, and a gate ships one repo's tree
              (ADR-2608124800)."
   :argv     ["nbb" "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-cljc-runtime-parity.cljs" "--root" "{{root}}" "--findings"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*\t"
   :interval-ms 86400000
   :timeout-ms  900000                  ; 15m -- ~24x the measured ~37s, all filesystem reads
   :writes   :none
   :measured "2026-08-30 by the orchestrator, re-run rather than taken from the building
              subagent's report: exit 1, ~37s wall.
              `SCANNED 4258 registered orgs/ checkouts present on disk and readable, of
              4290 registered under orgs/ (32 not checked out -> UNVERIFIED, not scanned)`.
              portable-claiming(.cljc)=3517; signal-a no-cljs-verification-path=2180, OF
              WHICH DOING BYTE WORK=318 (the error set) and 1862 :info; signal-b
              repos-with-hits=159, total-hits=352; unread-cljc-files=0.
              Discrimination checked in both directions against the JSON output, not the
              --findings text: the text output caps at 400 error lines sorted
              alphabetically, so it never reaches orgs/kotoba-lang/* and absence from it
              proves nothing -- I made exactly that mistake once while verifying this.
              Eight controls that DO have a cljs runner are absent from
              signal-a-error-repos: org-modbus, org-dnp3, org-amqp,
              org-lora-alliance-lorawan, org-can-cia-canopen, org-ethercat,
              com-profibus-profinet, org-ietf-diameter. org-ietf-srtp IS present, and its
              own README says the ClojureScript path was not cross-verified.
              Known limits, stated by the author: the walk covers src/ test/ tests/
              scripts/ script/ plus root package.json/nbb.edn/shadow-cljs.edn, so a runner
              in a nonstandard directory is missed and its repo is flagged undeservedly;
              the presence test is an OR of heuristics reverse-engineered from six naming
              conventions actually found here, and proves a runner EXISTS, never that it
              is executed; signal-b's bit-composition rule is a line-window regex, not a
              parser, and both misses and over-triggers."}

  {:id       :verify-protocol-lib-substance
   :title    "libraries that name a protocol and contain no wire code"
   :why      "2026-08-29. A survey asked which critical-infrastructure protocols this
              workspace was missing. Counting by repo name said almost none were: there
              is a repo for Modbus, OPC UA, ISO 8583, BGP, NMEA 2000, Fedwire. Reading
              them said otherwise. `com-modbus-tcp` is 182 lines of `entity-specs` +
              `routes` serving `POST /v1/requests` with zero bit or byte operations --
              it does not implement Modbus. `org-modbus`, 558 lines with 77 bitwise
              operations and a CRC checked two ways, does. Both look identical to every
              index this workspace owns: repo name, west manifest, concept index, and
              the maturity scorer, which reads bytes and scaffold markers and gives
              `com-modbus-tcp` `scaffold-marker-hits 0`.
              The measured shape of the problem: 1000 repos under orgs/kotoba-lang with
              a src/<name>/main.cljc of that form share only 231 distinct entity-name
              signatures. `com-nmea2000-marine` carries an EV-charging schema
              (ChargeSession/Connector/Trip) copied from an OCPP template; 47 payment
              repos including `com-fedwire` and `com-zengin-system` carry Stripe's
              object model (PaymentIntent/Payout/Refund). Fedwire is not
              PaymentIntent-shaped.
              This is the failure class CLAUDE.md names -- a check that could not
              measure returning the value of a check that measured and found nothing --
              applied to libraries rather than to gates. It cannot be a fleet gate: it
              reads orgs/, and a gate ships one repo's tree (ADR-2608124800).
              NOTE ON THE CLAIM SIGNAL: the phrase it keys on (`deep system protocol`)
              is itself template boilerplate, so a repo like `com-morningstar` asserts
              it implements a protocol only because the generator said so. The finding
              is still sound -- the repo does assert it and contains no wire code -- but
              the assertion is generated, not considered."
   :argv     ["nbb" "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-protocol-lib-substance.cljs" "--root" "{{root}}" "--findings"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*\t"
   :interval-ms 86400000
   :timeout-ms  900000                  ; 15m -- ~10x the measured ~63s, all filesystem reads
   :writes   :none
   :measured "2026-08-29 on this machine: exit 1, 302 findings, ~63s wall.
              `SCANNED 4230 registered orgs/ checkouts present on disk and readable, of
              4255 registered under orgs/ (25 not checked out -> UNVERIFIED, not
              scanned)` / `not-facade-shape=3206 facade-no-protocol-claim=722
              facade-claims-protocol=302 (clean=0 thin=0 no-wire-code=302)`.
              3206+722+302 = 4230 = SCANNED, so the buckets account for every repo it
              looked at, and the 25 it could not read are reported UNVERIFIED rather
              than folded into clean.
              Discrimination re-run by the orchestrator rather than taken from the
              subagent's report, ten probes, both directions watched: FLAGGED --
              com-modbus-tcp, com-opc-ua, com-iso-8583, com-nmea2000-marine,
              com-fedwire. NOT flagged -- org-modbus, org-ietf-tls, io-libp2p (real wire
              implementations, and no org-/io-/tech-/dev- repo is flagged at all), and
              com-stripe, com-adyen (facade-shaped with zero bit operations, but their
              README says `platform`, never `protocol`: a REST wrapper for a genuine
              REST platform is what correct looks like there).
              Known limit: no repo in this corpus is facade-shaped AND claims a protocol
              AND has real wire code, so the `do not flag an adequate implementation`
              branch is proven only indirectly, via org-modbus's different shape and
              com-stripe's different claim, not via an in-corpus boundary case."}

  {:id       :verify-single-page-app
   :title    "UI that cannot be opened as one page"
   :why      "2026-08-26, owner instruction: `uiux は singlepage app として見れる
              ようにしてね. これは skill, rule に組み込んで`. The rule already existed --
              ADR-2608080100 made single-page mandatory for kotoba-lang UI on
              2026-08-08 -- and nothing checked it. That ADR was itself written after
              two apps had shipped a second document for a secondary view: React, the
              cljs core and the whole design system compiled and shipped twice (3.6 MB
              across the pair, 2.1 MB as one page), and only one of the two app shells
              followed the DADS migration. The other went on linking a stylesheet that
              migration had deleted, unstyled, for three days. The measured failure was
              invisible in source: an app with two documents reads exactly like an app
              with one.
              First run, 2026-08-26: 11 multi-document and 15 no-document over 285
              repositories with a browser build config. Two of the eleven are the SSR/OG
              marketing surfaces the ADR names as out of scope and are accepted below.
              It cannot be a fleet gate: it reads orgs/, and a gate ships one repo's
              tree, so on a node it would find an empty workspace and never go green
              (ADR-2608124800)."
   :argv     ["nbb" "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-single-page-app.cljs" "--root" "{{root}}" "--findings"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*\t"
   :interval-ms 86400000
   :timeout-ms  900000                  ; 15m -- 6x the measured ~2m30s, all filesystem reads
   :writes   :none
   :measured "2026-08-26 on this machine: exit 1, 26 findings,
              `SCANNED 285 repositories with a browser build config, of 4231 registered
              checkouts` / `multi-document=11 no-document=15 (of 56 with :target
              :browser)`, ~2m30s wall.
              Discriminated five ways on a three-repo fixture, each direction watched:
              (a) one page each, and an :esm library with no page -- exit 0, zero
              findings, so a component library is not reported for having no document;
              (b) one repo grows a second script-loading document -- exit 1, exactly
              that repo reported; (c) that document removed and a different repo's only
              page deleted -- exit 1, no-document reported for that repo alone;
              (d) a 404.html added -- exit 0, because the rule REQUIRES one on a static
              host and reporting it would punish following the rule;
              (e) west.yml removed -- exit 2 with REFUSED, not a pass, so a run that
              could not tell a registered checkout from a stale one never reports clean.
              The no-document class asks for shadow-cljs `:target :browser`
              specifically. Measured without that narrowing it reported 232 of 285, and
              nearly all were correct by design -- a codec, a component library, a node
              target. A class that is 81% correct-by-design is noise, and noise is how a
              detector stops being read.
              Known blind spot, stated rather than implied: an `:esm` app that loses its
              only document is NOT caught, because :esm is also the target of every
              library. cloud-itonami/app-otent is such an app. multi-document catches
              :esm apps; no-document does not."
   :accepted {"multi-document:orgs/network-awai/cloud-murakumo"
              {:since "2026-08-26"
               :why "25 documents, but they are a marketing site and a blog --
                     public/advertise.html, public/docs.html, public/blog/*. This is the
                     SSR/OG exception ADR-2608080100 states in its own text: pages that
                     need crawlable markup are not bound by the app rule, and binding an
                     app and a marketing surface with one rule is the mistake the ADR
                     warns against."
               :clears-when "cloud-murakumo stops being a crawlable marketing surface,
                             or its app half is split into its own repository."}
              "multi-document:orgs/network-awai/cloud-itonami"
              {:since "2026-08-26"
               :why "18 documents: public/index.html plus industries, marketplace,
                     kaisya and their /ja locale twins. Same SSR/OG exception -- these
                     are indexed pages, and the locale split is a crawler-facing
                     decision, not a routing one."
               :clears-when "the itonami site stops needing crawlable per-locale
                             markup, or its app surfaces move out of the site repo."}}}
  {:id       :verify-model-alias-admission
   :title    "a model alias resolving to a name the app will not admit"
   :why      "2026-08-27: cloud-itonami Bots ran at 37.3% completion for a day
              (150 turns, 94 failed; 25 of the last 50 resident runs `model-mismatch`)
              while the CLI, the server and the gateway all looked healthy -- because
              they were. `murakumo-main` is an ALIAS, the fleet had repointed it, and
              `cloud-itonami-app` still admitted only \"Qwen3.8-27B-Q4_K_M.gguf\". Every
              turn generated fine over HTTP 200 and was then thrown away by
              `assert-response-model!`. Nothing said so anywhere a person would look;
              it surfaced only because someone asked whether the Bots were stable.
              The alias has two slots that LABEL DIFFERENTLY -- five probes on
              2026-08-27 06:05Z returned both `murakumo-main` and
              `qwen3.8-27b-throughput-b70` -- which is why some turns completed rather
              than none, and why one probe is worthless here: it reports clean half the
              time while half of production is refused. The fix enumerated the measured
              names, which is a floor and not a fix; the next redeploy that moves the
              suffix reproduces the outage exactly. This detector is what makes that
              cost one session-start line instead of a day."
   :argv     ["nbb" "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-model-alias-admission.cljs"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*"
   :interval-ms 21600000                 ; 6h -- it spends 5 inference calls per run
   :timeout-ms  300000                   ; 5m -- measured 75s, and each probe caps at 90s
   :writes   :none
   :measured "2026-08-27 on this machine: 75s at load 20.71, exit 0,
              `SCANNED\t1 alias\tmurakumo-main\tprobes=5\tserved=murakumo-main,qwen3.8-27b-throughput-b70`,
              0 findings. Both directions were watched against the LIVE gateway, not
              only in --self-test: `--accepted murakumo-main` (the set that was in
              place during the outage) exits 1 and names
              `qwen3.8-27b-throughput-b70` as refused, and the unmodified run exits 0.
              It refuses rather than reporting clean when it cannot measure -- no
              accepted set, no API key, or no probe naming a model all exit 2. That
              path fired for real on its first live run, when a `js->clj` on the
              Promise.all result made every status read nil against a gateway that had
              answered fine."}
  {:id       :recruit-vertical-registry
   :title    "a market-size ranking that lost its sources, or a vertical whose floor broke"
   :why      "2026-08-29: the owner asked for Recruit's SaaS/media portfolio to be
              rebuilt on cloud-itonami largest-market-first. The ordering input is the
              thing that rots. ADR-2607252200 already had to walk this back once for
              isco-*: a market figure with no source, no basis and no reference year
              reads exactly like a measured one, and a ranking built on it is a
              fabricated global total presented as a measurement -- the specific
              failure CLAUDE.md's system-dynamics rule names. This detector fails the
              registry the moment a :measured row loses its url / retrieved-at /
              sha256 / unit / reference-year / publisher, and reports mixed ranking
              bases (the 2026-08-29 registry ranks seven rows on 経済センサス 売上高
              and one, chuko-sha, on 年間商品販売額) as an INFO line rather than
              letting the mix stay silent. It also names the next vertical to build,
              which is what keeps the loop from re-deriving state each session."
   :argv     ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
              "{{repo}}/scripts/recruit-vertical-tick.cljs" "--findings" "--root" "{{root}}"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   ;; The floor pins the UNIT (`vertical`) and the ratio form, not just a positive
   ;; number -- a floor detects "measured nothing", never "measured the wrong set",
   ;; so the unit has to be part of what it pins.
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*/[0-9]+\tvertical"
   :interval-ms 86400000                 ; 24h -- the registry is policy; it changes slowly
   :timeout-ms  300000                   ; 5m -- measured 3s, 100x headroom for a loaded box
   :writes   :none
   :measured "2026-08-29 in the remote container (orgs/ absent, nbb 1.4.208). Every
              direction below was WATCHED against a mutated copy, not asserted. Baseline:
              a fabricated orgs/ tree with all 13 declared repos and a non-empty src/ in
              each exits 0. Then, one mutation at a time: deleting :market/source from
              kekkon exits 1 naming `source-missing/kekkon` and still prints SCANNED and
              RESULT (an earlier revision crashed here on a nil, exiting 1 with no
              evidence line at all -- a run that could not measure wearing the exit code
              of a run that found something); changing census-retail :unit to
              :billion-jpy exits 1 with `ranking-unit-mismatch` and prints the table
              headed unit=UNKNOWN (the earlier revision checked that :unit was PRESENT,
              never that the sources AGREED, and left a row three orders of magnitude
              mis-ranked with zero findings); typing :measurd on inshoku exits 1 with
              `market-status-unknown/inshoku` (it previously dropped a 13-trillion-yen
              vertical out of the ranking silently); repointing :registry/authority
              :scheme at kotoba://awai/ exits 1 with nine `authority-off-scheme` rows,
              8 verticals plus the capability (the check had hardcoded the prefix while
              its message quoted the declared one); setting :choba's resource to an
              https:// URL exits 1 (capabilities were never walked); emptying every
              src/ exits 1 with `repo-no-source` (an empty directory used to satisfy the
              check); and removing the isic-6810 checkout exits 0 with an INFO
              `fleet-unmeasured/sumai/...` plus a `NOT-MEASURED fleet` line naming sumai,
              with NEXT staying (none) -- a partial west checkout must not produce a
              confident red or steer NEXT. With no orgs/cloud-itonami at all it exits 2
              REFUSED after printing SCANNED, which is how it behaved on its first real
              run here. Two questions it does not answer -- whether biscuit is live and
              whether hyakka claims exist -- are printed every run as NOT-MEASURED lines
              rather than left to look like passes. Runtime 3s; the 5m timeout is 100x
              headroom for a loaded box. The runtime here is npm-installed nbb 1.4.208
              rather than a PATH nbb; on the workstation the registered argv resolves
              normally."}
{:id :itonami-actor-pnl
   :title "the P&L input for cloud-itonami actors, and the reason it does not close"
   :why "2026-08-29, ADR-2608291009 (BOT / KUMO / YATA) defines what `a bot that
