{:id :verify-hermes-agent-health
   :title    "the resident agent fleet's credentials, which died two days before anyone was told"
   :why      "2026-08-31: the OpenRouter key in ~/.hermes/auth.json's credential pool
              answered 401 `User not found.` on 2026-08-29 21:13 -- a revoked key,
              not an empty balance. The pool wrote last_status=exhausted with
              last_error_reset_at=null, which never retries and never clears, and
              nothing said so for 37 hours across 22 profiles. What the operator
              eventually saw named two innocent things: `Context length exceeded
              (510,398 tokens). Cannot compress further.` and Cloudflare 1101 on
              api.murakumo.cloud. Neither was true. conversation_loop.py:6030
              prints that sentence when compression makes NO PROGRESS at any size
              -- the same line appears in this machine's own logs at 8,214 tokens
              -- and it made no progress because the auxiliary compressor was
              401ing with everything else. Falling past the dead primary to the
              last fallback then handed 327k to a 262,144 ceiling behind a single
              shared llama.cpp queue, which is where the 1101 came from. The
              primary's real window is 1,310,720; it always fitted. One dead
              credential, two errors that both blamed something downstream, two
              days. Cannot be a fleet gate: its whole input is ~/.hermes on the
              operator workstation, which exists in no repo tree and on no node.
              It reads no secret -- the fingerprint comparison that actually
              proved the diagnosis by hand needs a Keychain read, and launchd
              cannot answer an unlock prompt (ADR-2607178000), so that half stays
              in the hand-run set rather than becoming a permanent :inconclusive."
   :argv ["nbb" "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-hermes-agent-health.cljs" "--findings"]
   :cwd :root
   :findings :protocol
   :writes :none
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*"
   :timeout-ms 120000
   :interval-ms 21600000
   :measured "2026-08-31: 902ms wall for 26 files across 23 profiles at load
              average 28, so the 120s timeout is 130x headroom; 6h because a
              credential dies between one turn and the next, not between one
              landed ADR and the next. All three exits checked before landing.
              0: --self-test, six assertions. 1: the live install, 6 findings.
              2: a missing --home, which refuses rather than reporting a healthy
              fleet from an absent one. The self-test earned its place on the
              first run -- it failed, because `get` on a raw JS object returns
              nil, so pool-findings reported every dead credential as healthy.
              That is the exact bug this detector exists to catch, written into
              the detector itself, and only the both-directions test saw it.
              Log classes carry a 48h window on purpose: without one the first
              401 ever logged is a finding forever, and a detector that cannot
              return to green is the silence it was built to break. Pool state
              carries no window -- `exhausted` is a fact about now.
              2026-08-31 addendum: it read only the LIVE errors.log, so the 48h
              window was really whatever survived rotation. Measured that day,
              with a desktop client flooding the log at ~140 lines/min, the
              root profile's oldest surviving line was 55 minutes old and one
              real finding had already been evicted -- reading errors.log.1
              and .2 brought it back. It now also prints LOGSPAN: how many
              profiles actually cover the window, and names the ones where
              rotation ate the rest. A young log and a rotated-away log both
              fall short of the window and only one is a problem, so they are
              reported separately; the fleet MINIMUM conflated them and read
              0.0h because one profile's log was a minute old.
              2026-08-31 addendum 2, from its first real run: the window lets a
              FIXED credential clear a finding but not a RESTARTED gateway, and
              a restart is how this is actually fixed -- the env loader marks a
              home applied after its first fetch attempt, success OR error, so
              one transient failure poisons a KeepAlive process for its whole
              life. Measured: three profiles held findings whose newest lines
              were 10:27, 19:24 and 11:23 while every running gateway had
              started at 12:22 or later; all three were about processes that no
              longer existed and nothing said so. Findings are now correlated
              with the profile's live gateway from `ps` alone (no env read, no
              secret): newer process -> warn, older -> keep, and NOT FOUND ->
              KEEP, because downgrading on a failed lookup turns 'I could not
