   :why      "scripts/hermes-cron-jobs/ exists because the live cron definitions
              live in ~/.hermes/profiles/<p>/cron/jobs.json, are terminal-local,
              and are in no repo -- its stated purpose is to make a new
              terminal's rebuild procedural. Measured 2026-09-04: 97 profiles
              carried cron jobs and 21 were in the ledger. A rebuild from it
              would have restored 21% of the fleet, and nothing said so.

              The ledger's own --check reports that correctly: it exits 1 with
              `STALE: ledger differs from live cron definitions`. Nothing ran
              it. It appeared in no detector registry, no cron job and no gate;
              the only references to it in the tree were its own README and its
              own output file. And it recorded generated_at=\"regenerated-on-run\"
              -- a literal, because --check compared the file byte for byte and
              a real timestamp would have fired on every run. That bought a
              working check at the price of a ledger nobody could date, so a
              stale registry read exactly like a current one. Both are fixed
              (--check now normalises the one field expected to differ), and
              this detector is the part that keeps looking.

              It counts and rates nothing: ledger coverage, jobs whose :script
              or :workdir resolves nowhere, jobs declaring no :model (so
              ADR-2608313500's model order does not reach them), and
              failure_streak. Cannot be a fleet gate for the same reason as
              verify-hermes-agent-health: its whole input is ~/.hermes on the
              operator workstation, which exists in no repo tree and on no
              node. It reads no credential -- job prompts are not printed, only
              counted."
