{:id :verify-x402-offer-domain
   :title    "a live x402 offer whose EIP-712 domain is not the token's own"
   :why      "`extra` in a 402 offer is the domain a buyer signs under. If it
              differs from what the token declares, the signature recovers a
              DIFFERENT address and the payment is rejected as a bad signature,
              which points at the buyer rather than at the offer that told it
              what to sign.
              This has happened twice on x402.nexus in one day. Introduced
              2026-09-01: both requirement builders emitted `USD Coin`
              unconditionally, so every base-sepolia listing named a domain its
              own token does not use. Fixed, deployed, verified. Then it came
              back the same day -- a later deploy shipped a build without the
              fix, main said `USDC` and the live offers said `USD Coin`, and
              nothing noticed until a client SDK was run against the live
              service and printed the domain it had been handed.
              Nothing else catches it. The offer is well formed and every field
              is individually plausible. The scheme in production use is
              `transaction`, which does not read `extra` at all, so nothing
              fails until the first `exact` payment. verify-advertised-surfaces
              checks that a path ANSWERS, not that what it answers is true.
              The comparison is against the counterparty: the token's own
              name() is read from the chain, never from a table kept here,
              because a table would drift exactly the way the offer did.
              Cannot be a fleet gate: it reads a live catalog and calls
              contracts over the network."
   :argv ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
          "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-x402-offer-domain.cljs" "--findings"]
   :cwd :root
   :findings :protocol
   :writes :none
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*"
   :timeout-ms 120000
   :interval-ms 21600000
   :measured "2026-09-01: 2 networks, a few seconds; 6h because any deploy can
              reintroduce it and the last one did.
              Discriminates: verified against a local mock serving the same
              catalog three ways. The token's real name exits 0; the mainnet
              name on a testnet token exits 1 with a finding naming both
              values; an offer with no domain exits 1.
              Its own first version scanned one network of two and exited 0 --
              the base listings are POST-only and answer a GET with 404 rather
              than a 402, so probing everything with GET measured half the
              estate and reported clean. It now uses the catalog's own method
              and refuses when fewer networks were measured than found."}

  {:id :verify-advertised-surfaces
   :title    "a discovery document advertising an endpoint the host does not serve"
   :why      "2026-09-01, three times in one session on x402.nexus. (1) Four
              endpoints were reported as already live because each answered
              200; the worker served its index page for every unrouted path, so
              a nonsense path returned the same bytes and only one of the four
              existed. (2) /.well-known/mcp.json served a well-formed manifest
              advertising zero tools -- the renderer was called in the
              JSON-to-EDN direction, found no string keys in an EDN map, and
              returned an empty model rather than an error. (3) A tool in that
              manifest reached its endpoint by fetching the worker's own
              origin; Cloudflare answered 522 and the tool returned that error
              text as its result, wrapped in a 200.
              The negative control is the whole detector. Checking that an
              advertised path answers is not enough, because in the first case
              every path answered. What separates a served path from an
              unserved one is that it differs from what the host says to a path
              nobody could have advertised, so the control is fetched first and
              a body-for-body match is a finding however healthy its status
              looks. A 404 alone is not a finding: a host that says no is
              telling the truth, and it counts only where a document promised
              that path.
              Cannot be a fleet gate: it reads live public documents and probes
              hosts over the network, neither of which exists in a shipped
              tree.
              A catch-all host is reported UNMEASURED, not red. A single-page
              app serves the same shell for every client-routed path by design
              and this workspace requires SPAs, so demanding a fix would be a
              standing red nobody should clear: yoro.etzhayyim.com redirects
              every path to aozora.app, which returns the same 2455 bytes for
              /search and for a path nobody routed. Those paths are unmeasured,
              and the UNMEASURED lines print with or without --findings so a
              clean exit cannot be read as every promise having been checked."
   :argv ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
          "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-advertised-surfaces.cljs" "--findings"]
   :cwd :root
   :findings :protocol
   :writes :none
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*"
   :timeout-ms 120000
   :interval-ms 21600000
   :measured "2026-09-01: 81 advertised paths across the 6 hosts in this
              workspace that publish a discovery document, in well under the
              120s timeout at load 27; 6h because a route can be removed by any
              deploy. The 6 come from requesting /llms.txt from all 425
              non-wildcard hosts in the surface index: ten answered, and www
              and alias names fold to five, plus itonami.cloud which is not in
              that index.
              Discriminates: verified against a local host serving both real
              shapes. A path advertised and answering 404 reports `absent`; the
              same path answering the index reports the host as UNMEASURED; a
              path with a distinct body is clean; and a POST-only path answers
              402 and is clean, which proves the method comes from the document
              rather than being assumed.
              Standing findings 2026-09-01, both confirmed by hand: two
              atproto.etzhayyim.com .well-known paths answer Cloudflare 1033
              (proxied hostname with nothing behind it), and
              mcp.etzhayyim.com/xrpc/com.etzhayyim.mcp.message does not resolve
              in DNS at all although orgs/kotoba-lang/app-agentgateway declares
              that host. atproto.etzhayyim.com is promised by etzhayyim.com's
              own llms.txt and claimed by no repo in the surface index.
              Every red this produced before it had controls was its own bug,
              six of them: GET-probing POST endpoints, a truncated placeholder
              prefix, fragment links, globs, reading an HTML index served at
              /openapi.json as if it were a discovery document, and a
              registrable-domain helper threaded with ->> so that every host
              compared equal and the same-site filter accepted everything. All
              six are self-test cases now."}

