  {:id       :verify-ship-builds-first
   :title    "ship paths that can send out an artifact nobody built"
   :why      "2026-08-31, twice in one day and both times to the person fixing it.
              In the morning a stale bundle went out over a settlement fix and put
              an inflated public revenue figure back. In the evening a build exited
              127 -- its compiler was not on PATH -- the ship ran as a separate
              command, and only the second exit code was read, sending out a bundle
              three and a half hours old. The ship guard checks the CHECKOUT's git
              state, not the artifact's, so a checkout perfectly in step with main
              can send out bytes that disagree with its own source while the guard
              stays green. Two independent properties keep a ship path honest: it
              builds first (so a failed build stops the ship), and that build goes
              through resource-guard (so it takes its turn -- a build has held that
              lock twelve minutes here while another session's retries were all
              refused). Measured across the tree: 58 ship scripts, 17 can ship
              unbuilt, 39 build outside the guard, 2 do both. Not a fleet gate: it
              reads `orgs/`, so a node shipped only one repo's tree would report
              clean forever."
   ;; Registered 2026-08-31 in an older shape (:script/:args/:exit) that this
   ;; home does not accept, and admission is fail-closed for the WHOLE registry:
   ;; from that day until 2026-09-01 every tick ended `ADMISSION FAILED` and not
   ;; one of the 60 detectors ran. Converted here, measured rather than guessed:
