   ;; writeFileSync / appendFileSync / mkdirSync / rm / rename calls.
   :writes   :none
   :interval-ms 43200000
   :timeout-ms  600000}

  {:id       :verify-itonami-run-path
   :title    "the run path the resident is configured to take, against the tree it would take it in"
   :why      "2026-09-02: cloud-itonami-app PR #270 landed as \"Guest wasm run path;
              leftover JVM aliases deleted (**not JVM-removed**)\" -- it deletes
              :server, :mcp and :cli from deps.edn and replaces the launchers with
              nbb hosts that load a wasm guest. The parenthetical is the finding:
              deleting a run path and porting what ran on it are indistinguishable
              from the tree, because in both cases the alias is gone.

              What separates them lives in a different file. The launchd plist
              dev.cloud-itonami.app still reads `clojure -M:server`. Production did
              not notice for one reason that is not a safeguard: ~/.cloud-itonami/
              current points at a release cut BEFORE #270, so the alias the plist
              names still exists in the release it names. The next release flips
              that, and a resident whose launch command does not resolve does not
              fail loudly -- launchd KeepAlive restarts it forever and the port
              stays closed.

              There is more than one such pointer, and they name different trees.
              The superproject's `.mcp.json` runs `clojure -M:mcp` -- the second
              alias #270 deleted -- in the west checkout under orgs/. That works
              today only because `west update` REFUSED to advance that checkout
              past another session's uncommitted files; the pin already points
              past it. This is ADR-2609012000's "three pointers to the app, all
              disagreeing" for the third time, and it keeps recurring because the
              only way anyone checks a pointer is by starting it. So this reads
              every configured entry point and resolves each against the tree IT
              names, rather than against one directory.

              Measured the day it landed, on three real trees rather than fixtures:
              the west checkout (pre-#270) and the running release are both green on
              all three legs; origin/main is red on all three. The registry says
              what the denominator is -- 407 routes / 208 commands generated from
              server.clj's own cond -- so `two routes served` is a number here and
              not a caveat in a commit message.

              CANNOT be a fleet gate: the target is a west-managed checkout under
              orgs/, which a gate's single-repo tree does not carry. It also reads
              a plist in the operator's home, which no node has."
   :argv     ["nbb" "--classpath" ".:scripts/nbb_compat"
              "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-itonami-run-path.cljs" "--findings"
              "--root" "{{root}}"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*"
   ;; Read 2026-09-02, not assumed: the script's only fs writes are mkdirSync /
   ;; writeFileSync / rmSync inside `fixture!` and `plist!`, which run under
   ;; --self-test alone and only beneath TMPDIR.
