  {:id       :verify-grammar-authority-floor
   :title    "a checker that reads lang/guest-grammar.edn to decide what is forbidden and passes when it is missing or forbids nothing"
   :why      "2026-09-07 (aiueos K16 Co-Scientist, silence-h10 elo 1600): checkers read
              the source-surface authority with `(:forbidden-heads grammar #{})` or
              `(if-not (exists? p) [] ...)`, so an authority that cannot be read is
              the same value as an authority that forbids nothing, and `every
              forbidden head is classified` is vacuously true over an empty set.
              verify-checker-floors cannot see this: its count regex accepts
              `{:forbidden 0 ...}` as evidence, and a MISSING file makes these
              checkers crash (exit 1), never pass. This detector copies each
              grammar-reading checkout three times (unmodified / every
              guest-grammar.edn deleted / every one rewritten with
              `:forbidden-heads #{}`), runs each scripts/*.cljs that reads
              `guest-grammar` in CODE (a comment-only mention is not a read --
              check-vendored-copies-fleet was a false red before that filter), with
              clojure/clj/java/gh stubbed to exit 64, and reports exit 0 on a
              mutated copy. Cannot be a fleet gate: the checkers live in
              kotoba-lang checkouts under orgs/."
   :argv     ["nbb" "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-grammar-authority-floor.cljs" "--findings" "{{root}}/orgs/kotoba-lang"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*"
   :interval-ms 86400000
   :timeout-ms  900000
   :writes   :none
   :measured "2026-09-07 12:xx: orgs/kotoba-lang SCANNED 4 (2 checkouts: kotoba-lang and
              its linked worktree lang-pure-wt; 2 .bb scripts counted unrunnable, bb
              is retired) FINDINGS 1 (exit 1): kotoba-lang/scripts/check-grammar-authority.cljs
              exits 0 with `:forbidden-heads #{}` and prints `stats {:forbidden 0 ...}`
              then `W0 PASS` -- the hypothesis, red on a real checker. On a DELETED
              grammar the same checker crashes (exit 1, fails, not a finding).
              check-docs is baseline-red on its own tree (generated-doc drift, same
              on the real checkout), so its columns are not evidence either way.
              Empty directory and a tree whose scripts/ never reads the grammar
              (aiueos worktree) both exit 2. Self-check 6/6: floorless fixture red
              on both variants, floored fixture clean, version-only fixture red on
              missing only, text mutation removes the fixture heads, baseline green,
              comment-only mention filtered. Each run copies ~11 MB x 3 per checkout
              into os.tmpdir() and removes it. SCANNED is over checkouts, not a
              workspace total."}

  {:id       :verify-git-dep-pin-reachability
   :title    "every :git/sha in a deps.edn, asked of the library it actually names"
   :why      "CLAUDE.md says it outright: west pins have a gate (verify-west-pins) and deps.edn
              pins have none. Two things hide in that gap. A pin can name a commit on a branch
              that was never merged -- which CLAUDE.md forbids by name and nothing was checking
              -- and a pin can be a real ancestor that is simply old, so a fix the library
              landed cannot reach the repo, because tools.deps takes the newest sha it is SHOWN.
              What justifies running it is a measurement, not a fear. 2026-09-09: 98 repos
              pinned io.github.kotoba-lang/json before d5137c7, the commit that restored
              read-str's trailing kwargs; 23 of them actually called it that way and every such
              call threw ArityException. The fix had been on json's main for a day. It was
              merged and it was not deployed, and nothing in the tree could tell those apart.
              The same run found two pins on unmerged branches, one of them in cloud-itonami-app,
              a live deploy repo: kotoba-lang/authentication at 188eb0e6, three commits ahead of
              and twenty-six behind that library's main. If that branch is deleted the build
              stops resolving, and nothing would have said so first.
              Adjacent: verify-source-path-pins asks whether a shadow-cljs :source-path checkout
              sits at the revision west.yml pins. This asks a different question of a different
              file -- whether a deps.edn's own :git/sha is reachable from the library's main at
              all. A repo can pass that one and fail this one."
   :argv     ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
              "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-git-dep-pin-reachability.cljs" "--findings" "--root" "{{root}}"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*\t"
   :interval-ms 86400000                 ; 24h -- it costs one GitHub call per suspect
   :timeout-ms  900000                   ; 15m -- 7.6x the measured runtime, sized against load
   :writes   :none
   :measured "2026-09-09, load 35.8: 118s wall. SCANNED 3876 deps.edn files, 6883 git
              coordinates, 463 distinct libraries. 4 findings (2 distinct facts -- one
              coordinate appears in a repo plus two copies of it), 2910 stale, 3596 unresolved,
              237 current, 32 cleared by asking GitHub.
              That last number is the reason this detector has two stages. Judged from local
              remote-tracking refs alone it reported 33 unreachable pins and THIRTY-ONE of them
              were false: the local ref was simply behind the library's real main, which
              CLAUDE.md warns about in as many words. A local suspicion is therefore re-asked
              of GitHub and only reported if the server agrees.
              Self-check, both directions. Red: with a `gh` stub on PATH returning the secondary
              rate limit's 403, all 36 suspects land in :unverified, the finding list empties,
              and the detector REFUSES with exit 2 rather than exiting 0 -- because a run that
              could not ask must not return the value of a run that found nothing. Green: with
              the real gh, exit 1 and the same 4 findings.
              One measured false-positive class is closed in the code: the coordinate NAME is
              not the repo address. kami-app-cad names io.github.kotoba-lang/cad and points its
              :git/url at kotoba-lang/kami-engine-cad, where the pinned commit exists and is
              healthy; resolving by name reported it as a commit that does not exist. When a
              :git/url is declared it is the address.
              A library with no local checkout is UNRESOLVED (3596 coordinates), which is
              reported at :info and is not clean. A shallow checkout answers ancestry wrongly
              and with authority, so it is refused rather than believed."}

{:id       :verify-itonami-surface-split
   :title    "the surfaces cloud-itonami-app handed to cli and apex, after it handed them over"
   :why      "2026-09-09: cloud-itonami-apex owns the four Workers that hold real
              itonami.cloud hostnames, and cloud-itonami-cli pins four tables the app
              generates. Neither split removed what it replaced, and nothing was
              comparing the two sides.

              Measured against both repositories' main: all four Worker copies left in
              cloud-itonami-app carried the SAME Worker name and the SAME custom_domain
              as the apex originals -- agent.itonami.cloud, hooks.itonami.cloud,
              app.itonami.cloud, mcp.itonami.cloud -- so a release run from the app tree
              would have replaced the live Worker rather than failing. Three copies were
              byte-identical; itonami-fleet-dispatch had ALREADY DRIFTED, its copy
              missing `capital` from the API route test, so shipping it would have taken
              /api/capital off app.itonami.cloud -- the ingress cloud-itonami-api sits
              behind -- while every check in both repositories stayed green. A release
              has no fast-forward check: the last tree to ship wins, and nothing was
              measuring which tree that was.

              This CANNOT be a fleet gate, and the reason is structural rather than
              incidental: answering needs THREE checkouts at once. A gate ships only the
              target repo's own tree, so the same check placed there can only report
              that the other side is missing. cloud-itonami-cli already carries
              scripts/verify-pinned-surface.cljs, which correctly returns exit 2 REFUSED
              when it cannot find the app -- and would return it forever on a node. A
              check that can never answer is worth no more than one that is always green.
              The superproject is the only checkout holding app, cli and apex together.

              Three families, each discriminated by its own named reason on 2026-09-09
              rather than by going red for some reason: cli-pin-drift fires when the
              app's generated table is edited and not the CLI's pin; ingress-pin-drift
              fires when the pinned agent-edge write table stops matching the Worker
              apex ships; ingress-pin-shape fires on an entry written as (str \"a\" \"b\"),
              which edn/read-string returns as a LIST without throwing, so a pin can
              read cleanly and still hand a non-string to a comparison that then simply
              never matches. worker-copy fires on the duplication itself and reports
              whether the two copies still agree, because 'byte-identical today' and
              'already drifted' are different amounts of trouble and the finding should
              say which one it found."
   :argv     ["nbb" "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-itonami-surface-split.cljs"
              "--findings" "--root" "{{root}}"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :writes   :none
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*"
   :interval-ms 21600000
   :timeout-ms  60000
   :measured {:as-of "2026-09-09" :runtime-s 0.26 :load-1m 14.4
              :comparisons 8 :findings 5
              :note "0.23/0.24/0.26s over three runs at load 14.4. The timeout is not a
                     multiple of that warm run: the work is a directory walk plus file
                     digests, so a cold page cache dominates, and 60s is sized for that
                     rather than for 12x a warm one. Six-hourly because a surface moves
                     on the scale of a landed PR, not a commit. The clean run against a
                     fixed tree makes 9 comparisons -- one more than the workspace made
                     when this was written, because the ingress pin did not exist yet and
                     an absent pin is deliberately not a finding. exit 2 was confirmed
                     against a root with no orgs/ rather than assumed."}}

  {:id       :verify-kotoba-require-declarable
   :title    "files that require a kotoba.lang.* namespace with nowhere to declare it"
   :why      "clojure.string resolves for free: it ships with Clojure, with ClojureScript and
              with babashka. kotoba.lang.text does not. The two are therefore NOT
              interchangeable in one way no source-level diff shows -- a file with no project
              file above it runs on clojure.string and cannot run at all on kotoba.lang.text.
              Measured 2026-09-09, and the reason this exists: the clojure.string migration
              rewrote 281 files across 53 repos into exactly that state, including
              `#!/usr/bin/env bb` scripts invoked directly in repos with no deps.edn anywhere.
              One of them, m365-archive's bin/gpg-unlock.cljc, is what lets git-annex push that
              archive non-interactively. All of them have been put back and the migration tool
              now asks the question per file, but the property outlives that one tool and
              nothing in the tree was checking it.
              ⚠ The first version of this detector reported the entire workspace CLEAN, because
              its walk did not stop at the repo boundary and found THIS superproject's own
              deps.edn above every file under orgs/. It was caught by planting a file that
              could not possibly resolve and watching the detector not report it. A second
              audit written in shell had the mirror-image bug: it tested directory membership
              with `printf '%s\\n' \"$decl\" | grep -qx \"$d\"`, and when a repo has NO project
              file at all $decl is empty, printf emits one empty line, and grep -qx '' matches
              it -- so the repos most certain to be broken were the ones reported clean.
              Adjacent: verify-git-dep-pin-reachability asks whether a pin a deps.edn NAMES is
              reachable. This asks whether there is a deps.edn to name it in at all."
   :argv     ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
              "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-kotoba-require-declarable.cljs" "--findings" "--root" "{{root}}"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\\t[1-9][0-9]*\\t"
   :interval-ms 86400000                 ; 24h
   :timeout-ms  900000                   ; 15m
   :writes   :none
   :measured "2026-09-09: 12,671 file/library uses of kotoba.lang.* under orgs/, 305 of which
              looked unresolvable in the checkout and were re-asked of each repo's main.
              Exit 0, zero findings -- AFTER the 281 repairs landed and the affected repos were
              re-fetched. Before them the same command reported 307, then 275; the green is a
              result, not a blind check.
              TWO STAGES, for the reason this workspace keeps rediscovering: the checkout is
              not the repo. 301 of the 305 suspects were cleared by asking main, every one of
              them a local checkout sitting behind a revert that had already landed. 4 could
              not be asked at all and are reported :unverified, which is neither clean nor a
              finding.
              A keyword is not a require: `:kotoba.lang.surface-status/as-of` is a map key, and
              matching it reported plugin-hermes as unable to resolve a namespace it never asks
              for. Excluded in the pattern.
              A file inside the library it requires is exempt -- kotoba-lang/text's own tests
              require kotoba.lang.text and need no declaration to do it.
              The 742 :info 'undeclared' uses have a project file above them that does not name
              the library. Often fine (transitive, or :local/root on a sibling), so it is a
              number to watch rather than a defect."}

  {:id       :verify-build-input-addressing
   :title    "every build input in this workspace, and whether it is addressed by content"
   :why      "ADR-2609092000, owner directive. A build is only as trustworthy as the identity
              of what it pulls in, and this workspace owns the machinery for content addressing
              -- IPLD, CIDs, kotoba.storage.verify/verifying-block-store, ADR-2607289500's
              ruling that identity is a semantic definition CID -- while the build uses none of
              it. Measured 2026-09-09 over 4,336 project files and 20,097 build inputs: 6,954
              are 40-hex :git/sha and addressed; 3,675 are a seven-hex PREFIX, 2^28 and
              grindable with a mutable :git/tag beside it, of which 3,673 are one value copied
              into nearly every deps.edn; 6,317 are :mvn/version, a name with no integrity
              field anywhere in the coordinate; 3,128 are a cross-repo :local/root, a path into
              whatever happens to be checked out. Roughly two thirds of this workspace's build
              inputs are named rather than addressed.
              Unison has no package at all -- a definition is the hash of its typed AST, so
              there is nothing to substitute. Nix declares outputHash before fetching, which is
              why its caches need no trust. Deno's lock carries a SHA-256 per fetched URL. Go
              adds a transparency log so a server cannot serve different bytes to different
              people. All four address the bytes.
              Adjacent: verify-git-dep-pin-reachability asks whether a pin a deps.edn NAMES is
              reachable from its library's main. This asks whether the coordinate is an address
              at all."
   :argv     ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
              "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-build-input-addressing.cljs" "--findings" "--root" "{{root}}"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\\t[1-9][0-9]*\\t"
   :interval-ms 86400000                 ; 24h
   :timeout-ms  900000                   ; 15m
   :writes   :none
   :measured "2026-09-09: 4,336 project files, 20,097 build inputs, ~50s. Exit 1 on the prefix
              class, with maven and cross-repo :local/root reported as standing counts at :info
              so each number moves visibly when its step lands.
              DISCRIMINATED THREE WAYS ON FIXTURE TREES BEFORE REGISTRATION: one prefix among
              601 inputs exits 1 and names it; the same tree with every sha written in full
              exits 0; a tree below the evidence floor exits 2 saying it could not measure.
              Fixing the fixtures exposed a real defect in the detector first -- it passed
              orgs, scripts and 70-tools to ripgrep unconditionally, and naming a directory
              that does not exist makes rg exit 2, which the detector correctly read as 'could
              not measure' and which therefore made every fixture unrunnable. It now passes
              only the directories that exist, and refuses if none do.
              ⚠ It measures the CHECKOUT, not the repos' mains. This workspace's checkouts are
              routinely behind, so a wave expanding prefixes on every main will not move this
              number until west brings the checkouts along. A count that has not moved is not
              evidence that nothing landed."}

